window.CL_TESTS={"s1": {"id": "s1", "title": "VARC Sectional 1", "source": "VARC 01 - 2026", "durationSec": 2400, "marking": {"MCQ": [3, -1], "TITA": [3, 0]}, "passages": {"P1": "[There] are future and emerging technologies that may have. . .a profound impact. Consider, for example, brain-computer interfaces. In case computers are connected directly to the brain, not only are behavioural characteristics subject to privacy considerations, but even one’s thoughts run the risk of becoming public, with decisions of others being based upon them. In addition, it could become possible to change one’s behaviour by means of such technology. Such developments, therefore, require further consideration of the reasons for protecting privacy. In particular, when brain processes could be influenced from the outside, autonomy would be a value to reconsider to ensure adequate protection.\n\nApart from evaluating information technology against current moral norms, one also needs to consider the possibility that technological changes influence the norms themselves. Technology thus does not only influence privacy by changing the accessibility of information, but also by changing the privacy norms themselves. . . . With future and emerging technologies, such influences can also be expected, and therefore, they ought to be taken into account when trying to mitigate effects.\n\nAnother fundamental question is whether, given the future (and even current) level of informational connectivity, it is feasible to protect privacy by trying to hide information from parties who may use it in undesirable ways. Gutwirth & De Hert argue that it may be more feasible to protect privacy by transparency – by requiring actors to justify decisions made about individuals, thus insisting that decisions are not based on illegitimate information. This approach comes with its own problems, as it might be hard to prove that the wrong information was used for a decision. Still, it may well happen that citizens, in turn, start data collection on those who collect data about them, e.g. governments. Such “counter(sur)veillance” may be used to gather information about the use of information, thereby improving accountability. The open-source movement may also contribute to the transparency of data processing. In this context, transparency can be seen as a pro-ethical condition contributing to privacy.\n\nIt has been argued that the precautionary principle, well-known in environmental ethics, might have a role in dealing with emerging information technologies as well. The principle would see to it that the burden of proof for the absence of irreversible effects of information technology on society, e.g. in terms of power relations and equality, would lie with those advocating the new technology. Precaution, in this sense, could then be used to impose restrictions at a regulatory level, in combination with or as an alternative to empowering users, thereby potentially contributing to the prevention of informational overload on the user side. Apart from general debates about the desirable and undesirable features of the precautionary principle, challenges to it lie in its translation to social effects and social sustainability, as well as its application to consequences induced by intentional actions of agents. Whereas the occurrence of natural threats or accidents is probabilistic in nature, those who are interested in improper use of information behave strategically, requiring a different approach to risk (i.e. security as opposed to safety). In addition, proponents of precaution will need to balance it with other important principles, viz., informed consent and autonomy.", "P2": "During testimony before the British House of Commons Parliamentary Committee on Science and Technology in 2004, Robert Edwards was asked whether it was possible to draw lines between eugenics and new developments in reprogenetic technologies. Here is Edwards's strange response: \"[I]t depends what we mean by eugenics. Eugenics was started in the 1870s by an English geneticist who had the welfare of mankind in his mind. The work became degraded after 1930, caused by the Nazis, but also by various other things where people were found not to be behaving themselves fully correctly in relation to the way they abused their children. . . . I think we can define what we want to do without using that term, and I think we can make it clear to people what we want to do without using that term.\"\n\nEdwards's generous description of Galton and his ambitions offers a remarkably peculiar rendition of eugenics' origins and rhetorical demise. He gently glosses over the racism and classism that were entwined with the founding of eugenics as a concept. . . . Galton viewed eugenics as the then-most-recent iteration of race science, whereby scientific theories and methods are used to give credence to—and justify social biases that produce—the unequal distribution of resources and opportunities that make upper-class, able-bodied whites seem inherently superior and others substandard. It is a turducken of white supremacy, ableism, and classism, served with a side dish of scientific jargon to make otherwise distasteful bigotry and intolerance seem appetising. Galton conceived of eugenics as using human intervention to accelerate natural selection, with the goal of marginalising and eventually eliminating inferior groups. Characterising his agenda as part of a broader concern for humankind's welfare is, then, rather disingenuous; most historians today certainly do not remember Galton in this way.\n\nThe point, of course, is that Robert Edwards was an apologist for eugenics as well as an enthusiast. . . . Not just an outside professional activity, eugenics also shaped how he conducted his research and clinical work. . . . This is what Edwards had in mind with his awkward response to the Parliamentary Committee's question on whether a line could be drawn. He attempted to extricate the science of eugenics from its ideological components, arguing that transparency in \"say[ing] exactly what you are trying to do\" could skirt the loaded nature of the term \"eugenics.\" This could, he believed, allow researchers to shape population traits without the appearance of becoming entangled in politics and history.\n\nBut the notion of scientists controlling human reproduction and, eventually, our evolution is inherently political. There can be no disentanglement; such attempts only obfuscate what is at stake. It is a Galtonian endeavour, and Galton saw no distinction between eugenics as a clinical technique, as politics, and as ideology. Edwards strongly aligned himself with this broader vision, saying in 1999 that \"[s]oon it will be a sin of parents to have a child that carries the heavy burden of genetic disease. We are entering a world where we have to consider the quality of our children.\"", "P3": "Beginning in the late 1970s, the Chicago School of Antitrust claims that it made a major contribution to making antitrust policy coherent and \"scientific\" by introducing basic economic concepts. It advanced both the Consumer Welfare Standard (a normative economic theory to segregate legitimate economic competition goals from \"value judgments\") and a basic positive microeconomic theory to show how much of the conduct previously considered anti-competitive was justified on \"efficiency\" grounds. Its contributions had a major impact on the federal judiciary in the United States and the antitrust enforcement agencies that spread Consumer Welfare throughout the globe.\n\nThe Post-Chicago-School economists have not challenged the Consumer Welfare Standard. Instead, the Post-Chicago School has asserted that the Consumer Welfare Standard is redeemable—correctable for many of the overstatements and conservative political conclusions of the Chicago School. Proponents of the Post-Chicago School are fond of advancing the narrative that in the late 1980s and during the 1990s, major advancements occurred in industrial organisation economics. Many of the earlier Chicago School conclusions required assumptions that were not evident from the Chicago School economists and were often not typical of the factual situations under scrutiny in antitrust cases. . . .\n\nThe New Brandeisians reject several of the basic premises of the economic analysis of the Chicago School and the Post-Chicago School. Specifically, they repudiate the Consumer Welfare Standard, they wish to restore antitrust's traditional social goals, and they offer new policy objectives for antitrust. . . . Finally, they dismiss the economic method of taking markets as primary and natural and then turning to government action only when a \"market failure\" is present.\n\nPerhaps the starkest contrast between the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools and the New Brandeisians is the New Brandeisian recognition that free markets are not naturally occurring. For the New Brandeisians, like the early institutional economists and the legal realists, markets are not independent and primary. The term \"free markets\" is ideological, not definitional, because no market is truly free of regulation. Indeed, some markets, such as those for wholesale or retail electricity, only exist because of regulation. Nor are markets analytically \"free\" prior to government or other social organisations. Markets cannot exist without secure property rights, contract rules, and criminal sanctions. Nor can markets exist without social norms for cooperation. However, these are only the most fundamental of regulatory requirements. The existence of corporations, limited liability, financial markets, credit, currency, unions, work rules, and many other prerequisites of modern markets require detailed legal regimes. . . .\n\nThus, there are no \"free markets\" and \"non-free markets.\" There are many diverse market organisations depending on how the law and governmental regulatory structures shape these markets. . . . Perhaps it is this presumption that markets function well that has been the stumbling block of the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools. That presumption is only advisable if the market itself cannot cure the defect. New Brandeisians recognise that all markets require some government intervention or oversight to function. Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools have ideological blinders to such recognition that markets must have \"rules of the game.\"", "P4": "In his first book, The Human Mind (1930), [Karl] Menninger defends psychiatry as a legitimate science. By its very nature, Menninger argues, psychiatry is concerned with criminal behaviour. According to Menninger, \"As a science dealing with the queer and untoward behaviour of human beings, psychiatry is naturally much concerned with crime\". Being a psychiatrist, then, Menninger was concerned with crime and the criminal justice system. But he was critical of the ways in which psychiatrics, i.e., \"the study of mental disease and its manifestations and its treatment,\" was applied to issues of criminal justice. More specifically, he lamented the fact that, \"instead of functioning to aid the legal profession,\" psychiatrists are forced to classify people \"as belonging on one side or the other of a hypothetical line of so-called 'sanity'.\" After serving on a committee appointed by the American Psychiatric Association, Menninger and his colleagues made several recommendations about how psychiatry can better serve the criminal justice system, chief among them is the following: \"The elimination of the […] terms 'insane' and 'insanity', and 'lunacy', and the exemption of the psychiatrist from the necessity of pronouncing upon concepts of religious and legal tradition in which he has no authority or experience, such as 'responsibility', 'punishment', and 'justice'\". Menninger and his colleagues made these recommendations in 1928. After that, Menninger's opposition to labelling people who suffer from behavioural disorders, including criminal behaviour, as \"insane\" and punishing them only grew stronger.\n\nIn The Crime of Punishment (1968), Menninger argues that all forms of punishment are cruel, and that criminal behaviour should be treated, rather than punished, in much the same way that behavioural disorders and mental illness are treated. According to Menninger's obituary in The New York Times, the book \"contributed to penal reforms in a number of states.\" A natural extension of Menninger's view that all forms of punishment are cruel is his opposition to capital punishment. The death penalty is a frequent topic covered by prison newspapers. . . . Menninger contributed a piece on the subject. . .titled \"Verdict Guilty–Now What?\" Menninger writes: \"Capital punishment is […] morally wrong. It has a bad effect on everyone, especially those involved in it. It gives a false sense of security to the public. It is vastly expensive. Worst of all it beclouds the entire issue of motivation in crime, which is so importantly relevant to the question of what to do for and with the criminal that will be most constructive to society as a whole. Punishing–and even killing–criminals may yield a kind of grim gratification […]. But playing God in this way has no conceivable moral or scientific justification.\" In other words, Menninger argues that both consequentialist and retributivist justifications of capital punishment fail. . . . Menninger argues that from a consequentialist point of view, the death penalty cannot be justified because it does not bring about a balance of good over bad consequences for those involved in it. . . . From a retributivist point of view, Menninger argues, the death penalty cannot be justified, either, since no crime can render the criminal who committed it deserving of the death penalty."}, "passageExplanations": {}, "questions": [{"n": 1, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "The author would not disagree with each of the following statements EXCEPT:", "options": ["transparency may yield better results than precautionary principle transparency in dealing with the consequences of emerging information technologies.", "proponents of the precautionary principle must carefully balance its application with other important ethical principles to ensure comprehensive protection.", "translating the precautionary principle to social effects and sustainability is complex, particularly when dealing with intentional misuse of information.", "precautionary principle could be applied to emerging information technologies to ensure the absence of irreversible societal impacts."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 54, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation:\nThe author would not disagree with each of the following statements EXCEPT:\nThis question tests the ability to identify which statement the author would disagree with, essentially looking for a statement that contradicts the author's arguments or claims. The passage discusses the implications of emerging technologies, particularly brain-computer interfaces, and evaluates approaches like transparency and the precautionary principle to address privacy and ethical concerns.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 1: transparency may yield better results than precautionary principle transparency in dealing with the consequences of emerging information technologies.\nThe passage does discuss both transparency and the precautionary principle as potential solutions for handling the ethical and privacy concerns posed by emerging technologies. However, the author does not compare the efficacy of transparency against the precautionary principle directly or suggest that one is superior to the other. Instead, the passage highlights the strengths and limitations of both approaches. Transparency is noted for its potential to improve accountability and contribute to privacy, while the precautionary principle is seen as a way to impose restrictions and prevent irreversible societal impacts. Since the author does not assert that transparency yields better results than the precautionary principle, this statement is something the author would likely disagree with.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 2: Proponents of the precautionary principle must carefully balance its application with other important ethical principles to ensure comprehensive protection.\nThe passage explicitly mentions that the precautionary principle should be balanced with other ethical principles, such as informed consent and autonomy. This indicates that the author agrees with this view, making this statement something the author would not disagree with.\nOption 3: Translating the precautionary principle to social effects and sustainability is complex, particularly when dealing with intentional misuse of information.\nThe passage discusses the complexities of applying the precautionary principle to social effects and the strategic behaviour of those who misuse information. This statement accurately reflects the author's discussion of these challenges, indicating agreement.\nOption 4: Precautionary principle could be applied to emerging information technologies to ensure the absence of irreversible societal impacts.\nThe passage suggests that the precautionary principle could indeed be used to prevent the irreversible effects of information technology on society, particularly in terms of power relations and equality. The author discusses the role of this principle in regulating emerging technologies, so this statement aligns with the author's views."}, {"n": 2, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "It can be inferred from the passage that the brain-computer interface is an example of how emerging information technologies:", "options": ["can be misused to change the behavioural characteristics of human beings.", "can change behavioural characteristics to a point where it is homogenous.", "impact the morals that are widely accepted in society today.", "has a profound impact on the way in which we characterise technology."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 16, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation:\nIt can be inferred from the passage that the brain-computer interface is an example of how emerging information technologies:\nThis question requires us to infer how brain-computer interfaces (BCIs), as an example of emerging information technologies, interact with ethical standards and societal norms. The goal is to identify which option best captures the essence of the impact of BCIs discussed in the passage.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 3: impact the morals that are widely accepted in society today.\nThe passage discusses how BCIs present significant ethical considerations, especially concerning privacy and autonomy. In the first paragraph, the author explains that BCIs could make one's thoughts public and influence behaviour, thereby challenging traditional notions of privacy and autonomy. The second paragraph explicitly states, \"Apart from evaluating information technology against current moral norms, one also needs to consider the possibility that technological changes influence the norms themselves.\" This statement confirms that BCIs have a direct impact on the morals that are widely accepted in society today, making option 3 the best fit.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: can be misused to change the behavioural characteristics of human beings.\nThe passage does mention that BCIs could change behaviour (\"it could become possible to change one's behaviour by means of such technology\"), but it does not frame this in terms of misuse. The focus is on the broader ethical implications, such as privacy and autonomy, not solely on misuse to change behaviour.\nTherefore, this option is inaccurate.\nOption 2: can change behavioural characteristics to a point where it is homogenous.\nThis option is not supported by the passage. The passage discusses the potential for BCIs to change behaviour but does not imply that these changes would lead to homogeneity in behaviour. The idea of homogeneity is not mentioned or suggested in the text.\nOption 4: has a profound impact on the way in which we characterise technology.\nThis option misinterprets the focus of the passage. The discussion is centred around the ethical and societal implications of BCIs, not on how we characterise technology itself. The passage focuses on privacy, autonomy, and the need to re-evaluate ethical standards, rather than the characterisation of technology."}, {"n": 3, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "Each of the following is true about transparency EXCEPT that it:", "options": ["promotes ethical behaviour.", "can be a form of accountability.", "protects from illegitimate information.", "allows for greater scrutiny."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 34, "solution": "Question Explanation: Each of the following is true about transparency EXCEPT that it: This question asks us to identify which statement about transparency is NOT supported by the passage. We need to review the third paragraph and see which aspects of transparency are discussed and which are not.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 3: protects from illegitimate information. The passage states that transparency involves requiring actors to justify decisions made about individuals, insisting that decisions are not based on illegitimate information. However, it also mentions the challenge of proving that wrong information was used for a decision. Transparency does not inherently protect from illegitimate information; it requires justification for decisions but does not guarantee protection from the use of such information.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: promotes ethical behaviour. The passage mentions that transparency can be seen as a \"pro-ethical condition,\" contributing to ethical behaviour by making decision-making processes open and justifiable. This suggests that transparency promotes ethical behaviour by ensuring that decisions are based on legitimate and justifiable grounds.\nOption 2: can be a form of accountability. The passage discusses how transparency can lead to accountability by requiring actors to justify their decisions and by enabling counter-surveillance, where citizens collect data on those who collect data about them. This creates a system where decision-makers are held accountable for their actions, ensuring that decisions are made transparently and justifiably.\nOption 4: allows for greater scrutiny. The passage implies that transparency allows for greater scrutiny by making decision-making processes open to examination and requiring actors to justify their decisions. It also mentions the role of counter-surveillance and the open-source movement in enhancing transparency, thereby increasing the scrutiny of data processing practices."}, {"n": 4, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "Which one of the following is not an illustration of the concerns raised in the passage?", "options": ["the introduction of blockchain technology leads to greater transparency in financial transactions, prompting a reevaluation of privacy norms.", "the proliferation of smart home devices results in new regulations to protect users' privacy.", "the widespread use of facial recognition technology in public places leads to changes in societal norms about what constitutes acceptable surveillance.", "advances in brain-computer interface technology allow for more accurate diagnoses, but reduce the amount of direct interaction between patients and doctors."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 61, "solution": "Question Explanation: Which one of the following is not an illustration of the concerns raised in the passage? This question requires us to identify which scenario does NOT illustrate the concerns raised in the passage. The passage discusses concerns related to privacy, ethical implications, autonomy, transparency, and the influence of emerging technologies on societal norms.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 4: advances in brain-computer interface technology allow for more accurate diagnoses, but reduce the amount of direct interaction between patients and doctors. This scenario focuses on the medical implications of brain-computer interface technology, specifically the accuracy of diagnoses and the reduction in direct interaction between patients and doctors. While the passage discusses privacy and ethical concerns related to brain-computer interfaces, it does not address the impact on the doctor-patient interaction.\nTherefore, this option does not illustrate the concerns raised in the passage.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: the introduction of blockchain technology leads to greater transparency in financial transactions, prompting a reevaluation of privacy norms. This scenario relates to transparency and the reevaluation of privacy norms, which are key concerns discussed in the passage. The passage emphasises how emerging technologies influence privacy norms and ethical standards, making this option relevant to the concerns raised.\nOption 2: the proliferation of smart home devices results in new regulations to protect users' privacy. This scenario addresses the privacy concerns associated with emerging technologies, a major theme in the passage. The passage discusses the need to protect privacy and the potential for new regulations to address these concerns.\nTherefore, this option illustrates the concerns raised in the passage.\nOption 3: the widespread use of facial recognition technology in public places leads to changes in societal norms about what constitutes acceptable surveillance. This scenario aligns with the passage's discussion on how emerging technologies can influence and change societal norms, especially concerning privacy and surveillance. The passage mentions that technological changes can lead to changes in moral and societal norms, making this option relevant to the concerns raised."}, {"n": 5, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "Which of the following is the main criticism that the author has towards Edwards's views on eugenics?", "options": ["Edwards argues that modern reprogenetic technologies can be pursued without invoking eugenics.", "Edwards believes that transparency in scientific goals can mitigate the negative connotations of eugenics.", "Edwards fails to acknowledge the original scientific intentions behind the concept of eugenics.", "Edwards attempts to separate the science of eugenics from its historical and ideological roots."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 63, "solution": "Correct Answer – (4)\nQuestion Explanation: Which of the following is the main criticism that the author has towards Edwards's views on eugenics? This question asks us to identify the main criticism that the author has towards Edwards's views on eugenics. The passage discusses Edwards's attempt to differentiate modern reprogenetic technologies from the historically negative connotations of eugenics and the author's disagreement with this attempt.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 4: Edwards attempts to separate the science of eugenics from its historical and ideological roots. The main criticism in the passage is directed at Edwards's effort to dissociate the scientific aspects of eugenics from its historical and ideological components. The author argues that this separation is not only impossible but also disingenuous. The passage indicates that eugenics, as conceived by Galton and others, is deeply intertwined with racism, classism, and ableism, and these elements cannot be extricated from the concept. The author criticises Edwards for glossing over these problematic aspects and attempting to sanitise eugenics by focusing solely on its scientific goals. This makes option 4 the best fit, as it accurately reflects the main point of the author's critique.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: Edwards argues that modern reprogenetic technologies can be pursued without invoking eugenics. While Edwards does argue this point, it is not the core of the author's criticism. The author's main issue is not merely with the pursuit of modern reprogenetic technologies but with Edwards's attempt to separate these technologies from the historical and ideological context of eugenics. This option only partially captures the author's critique.\nOption 2: Edwards believes that transparency in scientific goals can mitigate the negative connotations of eugenics. This statement is part of Edwards's argument, but it is not the main focus of the author's criticism. The author does discuss Edwards's belief in transparency, but the primary critique is that Edwards attempts to separate the science of eugenics from its historical and ideological roots.\nTherefore, this option misses the broader context of the author's argument.\nOption 3: Edwards fails to acknowledge the original scientific intentions behind the concept of eugenics. This option is incorrect because Edwards does acknowledge the origins of eugenics but presents them in a positive light, glossing over the problematic aspects. The main criticism from the author is that Edwards tries to separate science from its historical and ideological roots, not that he fails to acknowledge its origins."}, {"n": 6, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "The author believes that:", "options": ["Galton, unlike Edwards, embraced eugenics as both an ideological and scientific pursuit.", "Galton, unlike Edwards, believed in the power of scientific intervention in human reproduction.", "Galton, like Edwards, had little regard for the ethical implications of their work.", "Galton, like Edwards, sought to distance himself from the negative connotations of eugenics."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 61, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation: The author believes that: The question asks us to determine which statement accurately reflects the author's beliefs about the similarities and differences between Galton and Edwards regarding eugenics. The options focus on their attitudes towards eugenics, scientific intervention in human reproduction, ethical considerations, and attempts to distance themselves from negative connotations.\nCorrect Answer Explanation: Option (1): Galton, unlike Edwards, embraced eugenics as both an ideological and scientific pursuit. The passage clearly outlines that Galton viewed eugenics as an integrated ideological and scientific endeavour. He saw no separation between the clinical application, politics, and ideology of eugenics, meaning he embraced the whole spectrum of eugenics as a pursuit. On the other hand, Edwards attempted to separate the scientific aspects of eugenics from its ideological components, suggesting that clear communication about scientific goals could sidestep the controversial term \"eugenics.\"\nIncorrect Answer Explanations: Option (2): Galton, unlike Edwards, believed in the power of scientific intervention in human reproduction. This is incorrect because both Galton and Edwards believed in scientific intervention in human reproduction. The passage indicates that Galton conceived of eugenics as using human intervention to accelerate natural selection. Edwards, too, advocated for the selection against genetic diseases, as evidenced by his 1999 statement about considering the quality of children. Option (3): Galton, like Edwards, had little regard for the ethical implications of their work. This option is not supported by the passage. While the author strongly implies Galton's lack of ethical consideration, it is not as explicit about Edwards' ethical stance. Edwards' attempts to avoid the term \"eugenics\" and focus on scientific goals could be interpreted as an ethical consideration. However, it does not confirm that Edwards had little regard for ethics in the same explicit manner it does for Galton. Option (4): Galton, like Edwards, sought to distance himself from the negative connotations of eugenics. This option is incorrect because the passage states that Galton did not distinguish between eugenics as a scientific technique, politics, and ideology. He did not seek to distance himself from the term or its connotations. Edwards, conversely, attempted to separate science from its ideological baggage by avoiding the term \"eugenics\" and emphasising transparency in scientific aims."}, {"n": 7, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "What does Robert Edwards imply by the statement, \"[s]oon it will be a sin of parents . . . we have to consider the quality of our children.”?", "options": ["Parents will face societal pressure to use genetic technologies to prevent their children from having genetic diseases.", "The advancements in genetic technologies will allow parents to avoid having children with genetic diseases.", "Parents will be morally obligated to use genetic technologies to ensure their children are free from genetic diseases.", "Parents who do not use genetic technologies to prevent genetic diseases in their children will be viewed negatively."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 45, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation: What does Robert Edwards imply by the statement, \"[s]oon it will be a sin of parents . . . we have to consider the quality of our children.”? The question asks us to interpret what Robert Edwards implies by his statement, \"[s]oon it will be a sin of parents . . . we have to consider the quality of our children.” This requires us to delve into the implications of Edwards's view on the moral responsibilities of parents in the context of genetic technologies.\nCorrect Answer Explanation: Option (3): Parents will be morally obligated to use genetic technologies to ensure their children are free from genetic diseases. Edwards's use of the word \"sin\" conveys a strong moral judgment, implying that parents would be committing a moral wrong by not using genetic technologies to prevent genetic diseases in their children. The phrase \"we have to consider the quality of our children\" indicates that ensuring children are free from genetic diseases will not just be a matter of choice but a significant ethical duty for parents. It suggests that the quality of a child's genetic makeup will become an essential consideration, and neglecting this responsibility would be seen as morally reprehensible. Edwards's statement implies a shift in societal values where the use of genetic technologies will be seen as a moral imperative, making it unethical for parents to knowingly allow genetic diseases to be passed on to their children.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations: Option (1): Parents will face societal pressure to use genetic technologies to prevent their children from having genetic diseases. While societal pressure can be a component of moral obligation, Edwards's use of \"sin\" indicates a stronger ethical mandate rather than just societal expectations. Societal pressure may influence behaviour, but it does not necessarily imply a moral obligation as strongly as the concept of sin does. Option (2): The advancements in genetic technologies will allow parents to avoid having children with genetic diseases. This option focuses on the capabilities provided by genetic advancements but does not address the moral implications implied by Edwards's statement. It describes the potential of genetic technologies without touching on the ethical responsibility that Edwards suggests parents will have. Option (4): Parents who do not use genetic technologies to prevent genetic diseases in their children will be viewed negatively. This option highlights the potential social stigma against parents who do not use genetic technologies. However, it does not fully capture the moral imperative that Edwards is suggesting. While negative views from society may arise, the concept of it being a \"sin\" suggests a deeper moral and ethical obligation rather than just societal disapproval."}, {"n": 8, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "By Galtonian endeavour, the author implies each of the following EXCEPT:", "options": ["an effort to justify social biases through scientific theories.", "a political agenda intertwined with scientific pursuits.", "a movement to prioritise genetic technologies over others.", "an attempt to marginalise inferior groups through human intervention."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 58, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation: By Galtonian endeavour, the author implies each of the following EXCEPT: The question asks us to identify which statement is NOT implied by the author's use of the term \"Galtonian endeavour.\" This involves understanding the context in which the author discusses Galton's approach to eugenics and the characteristics attributed to it.\nCorrect Answer Explanation: Option (3): a movement to prioritise genetic technologies over others. The term \"Galtonian endeavour\" refers to Francis Galton's approach to eugenics, which involved using scientific theories to justify social biases, intertwining political agendas with scientific pursuits, and attempting to marginalise inferior groups through human intervention. The author does not suggest that Galton's approach was about prioritising genetic technologies over others. Instead, the focus is on how Galton used eugenics to justify existing social hierarchies and biases, and his broader political and ideological agenda.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations: Option (1): an effort to justify social biases through scientific theories. This is implied by the author, who explains that Galton used eugenics to give scientific credence to social biases and inequalities, thereby justifying the unequal distribution of resources and opportunities. Option (2): a political agenda intertwined with scientific pursuits. This is also implied by the author, who mentions that Galton did not distinguish between eugenics as a clinical technique, politics, and ideology. His scientific pursuits were deeply intertwined with a political agenda aimed at social engineering. Option (4): an attempt to marginalise inferior groups through human intervention. This is clearly implied by the author, who states that Galton aimed to use human intervention to accelerate natural selection, with the goal of marginalising and eventually eliminating groups he deemed inferior."}, {"n": 9, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "The likely consequence of adopting the New Brandeisian perspective on antitrust policy, as opposed to the Chicago School's perspective, is:", "options": ["shift towards treating markets as naturally occurring phenomena.", "increased emphasis on consumer welfare and economic efficiency as primary goals.", "lesser freedom for the market itself in ensuring market fairness and social goals.", "subjective considerations are seen as less appropriate for economic policy decisions."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 57, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation The likely consequence of adopting the New Brandeisian perspective on antitrust policy, as opposed to the Chicago School's perspective, is: This question asks about the likely consequences of adopting the New Brandeisian perspective on antitrust policy, as compared to the Chicago School's perspective. It requires understanding the key differences between the New Brandeisian approach and the Chicago School's approach, as described in the passage.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (3): lesser freedom for the market itself in ensuring market fairness and social goals. This is the correct answer because the New Brandeisians reject the idea that markets are naturally occurring and emphasise the need for regulatory frameworks to ensure market fairness and achieve social goals. The passage highlights that the New Brandeisians believe that all markets require some form of government intervention or oversight, contrasting with the Chicago School's belief in the natural functioning of markets with minimal intervention. Thus, adopting the New Brandeisian perspective would likely result in lesser freedom for the market itself to ensure fairness and social goals, as more regulatory oversight would be deemed necessary.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (1): shift towards treating markets as naturally occurring phenomena. This is incorrect because it directly contradicts the New Brandeisian perspective. The passage explicitly states that the New Brandeisians do not see markets as naturally occurring phenomena but rather as constructs that require regulation and oversight to function properly. Option (2): increased emphasis on consumer welfare and economic efficiency as primary goals. This is incorrect because it aligns more with the Chicago School's perspective, which emphasises the Consumer Welfare Standard and economic efficiency. The New Brandeisians, on the other hand, reject several premises of the Chicago School, including the Consumer Welfare Standard, and instead focus on restoring traditional social goals and new policy objectives for antitrust. Option (4): subjective considerations are seen as less appropriate for economic policy decisions. This is incorrect because the New Brandeisians do not argue for reducing subjective considerations in economic policy decisions. In fact, they reject the Chicago School's narrow focus on economic efficiency and Consumer Welfare Standard, advocating instead for broader social goals, which inherently involve more subjective considerations."}, {"n": 10, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "Which of the following best describes a commonality between the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools, as well as a primary difference between them?", "options": ["Both schools accept the Consumer Welfare Standard; however, the Post-Chicago School favours traditional social goals.", "Both schools agree on the importance of economic efficiency; however, the Post-Chicago School emphasises an analysis that considers actual market conditions.", "Both schools advocate for minimal government intervention; however, the Post-Chicago School supports the value judgements over normative economic theory.", "Both schools maintain that markets function best with little regulation; however, the Post-Chicago School seeks to prioritise economic efficiency as a primary goal."], "correct": "B", "cl_accuracy": 48, "solution": "Correct Answer – (2)\nQuestion Explanation Which of the following best describes a commonality between the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools, as well as a primary difference between them? This question requires identifying a commonality between the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools and distinguishing a primary difference between them, as outlined in the passage.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (2): Both schools agree on the importance of economic efficiency; however, the Post-Chicago School emphasises an analysis that considers actual market conditions. This is the correct answer. Both the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools value economic efficiency as a fundamental principle. The passage notes that the Chicago School advanced the Consumer Welfare Standard and justified much previously considered anti-competitive conduct on \"efficiency\" grounds. The Post-Chicago School, while not challenging the Consumer Welfare Standard, suggests that the analysis should account for actual market conditions, implying a need for more nuanced and context-specific assumptions than those typically used by the Chicago School.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (1): Both schools accept the Consumer Welfare Standard; however, the Post-Chicago School favours traditional social goals. This is incorrect. While it is true that both schools accept the Consumer Welfare Standard, the passage does not indicate that the Post-Chicago School favours traditional social goals. Instead, the Post-Chicago School seeks to refine the Consumer Welfare Standard and correct the overstatements of the Chicago School, but it does not advocate for traditional social goals, which is a stance more aligned with the New Brandeisians. Option (3): Both schools advocate for minimal government intervention; however, the Post-Chicago School supports the value judgements over normative economic theory. This is incorrect. The passage does not state that either school supports value judgments over normative economic theory. While both schools might lean towards minimal government intervention, the primary difference lies in the Post-Chicago School's emphasis on actual market conditions rather than supporting value judgments. Option (4): Both schools maintain that markets function best with little regulation; however, the Post-Chicago School seeks to prioritise economic efficiency as a primary goal. This is incorrect. Although both schools might generally support minimal regulation, the primary difference is not about prioritising economic efficiency. The Post-Chicago School continues to prioritise economic efficiency but emphasises a more detailed analysis considering actual market conditions, not merely prioritising economic efficiency over other aspects."}, {"n": 11, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "New Brandesians would most likely consider government intervention as:", "options": ["necessary to ensure markets do not remain free.", "key to creating a legal regime to control the markets.", "cental to safeguarding consumer welfare.", "integral to ensuring markets function effectively."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 57, "solution": "Correct Answer – (4)\nQuestion Explanation New Brandesians would most likely consider government intervention as: This question asks about the New Brandeisians' view on government intervention in markets, based on their perspective as described in the passage.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (4): integral to ensuring markets function effectively. This is the correct answer because the New Brandeisians believe that markets require some form of government intervention or oversight to function properly. The passage states that the New Brandeisians recognise that all markets need government intervention to ensure they operate effectively. They argue that markets cannot exist without regulations and legal frameworks, indicating that such intervention is integral to market functionality.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (1): necessary to ensure markets do not remain free. This is incorrect because the New Brandeisians' stance is not about ensuring markets do not remain free, but rather about recognising that markets are not naturally free and need regulation to function. The focus is on the necessity of regulation for effective market operation, not on preventing freedom. Option (2): key to creating a legal regime to control the markets. This is incorrect because the emphasis of the New Brandeisians is not solely on controlling markets through legal regimes but on ensuring markets function effectively through appropriate regulations and oversight. While legal regimes are part of this, the primary focus is broader. Option (3): central to safeguarding consumer welfare. This is incorrect because safeguarding consumer welfare is more aligned with the Chicago and Post-Chicago Schools, which focus on the Consumer Welfare Standard. The New Brandeisians are concerned with broader social goals and the overall functionality of markets through regulation, rather than prioritising consumer welfare specifically."}, {"n": 12, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "Each of the following statements, if true, strengthens the argument of New Brandesians EXCEPT:", "options": ["government regulation is an ideological solution to market woes.", "excessive centralised private power is dangerous for society.", "\"excessive regulation\" is an illusory and misleading category.", "how the law is coded determines how wealth is created in markets."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 34, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation Each of the following statements, if true, strengthens the argument of New Brandesians EXCEPT: This question requires identifying the statement that does not strengthen the argument of the New Brandeisians. Each option must be evaluated to determine if it supports or undermines the New Brandeisian perspective on government intervention and market regulation.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (1): government regulation is an ideological solution to market woes. This option does not strengthen the New Brandeisian argument because it frames government regulation as merely an ideological solution rather than a practical or necessary one. The New Brandeisians argue that government intervention is integral to the proper functioning of markets, not just an ideological stance. This framing could suggest that government regulation is not based on practical necessity but on a specific ideology, which weakens their argument.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (2): excessive centralised private power is dangerous for society. This strengthens the New Brandeisian argument because it supports their view that unchecked market power can be harmful and requires government intervention to prevent such dangers, aligning with their emphasis on the need for regulatory oversight. Option (3): \"excessive regulation\" is an illusory and misleading category. This strengthens the New Brandeisian argument by challenging the notion that too much regulation is inherently bad. By arguing that the concept of \"excessive regulation\" is misleading, it supports the idea that robust regulation is necessary and beneficial, countering claims from the Chicago School that minimal regulation is preferable. Option (4): how the law is coded determines how wealth is created in markets. This strengthens the New Brandeisian argument by emphasising the importance of legal frameworks in shaping market outcomes. It aligns with their view that markets require detailed legal regimes to function properly and that these regimes significantly influence economic activity and wealth creation."}, {"n": 13, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "Which of the following assumptions does Menninger's argument against the use of the term 'insane' rely on?", "options": ["The term has legal implications that psychiatrists are not qualified to address.", "Psychiatry is concerned with treatment rather than aiding the legal profession.", "There is no clear definition of 'insane' that can be consistently applied.", "People labelled as 'insane' are often subjected to unjust punishments."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 39, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation Which of the following assumptions does Menninger's argument against the use of the term 'insane' rely on? This question requires identifying the assumption underlying Menninger's argument against the use of the term \"insane\" in the context of psychiatry and criminal justice.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (1): The term has legal implications that psychiatrists are not qualified to address. This is the correct answer. Menninger's argument against using the term \"insane\" is based on the idea that it forces psychiatrists to make legal judgments they are not equipped to handle. The passage states that Menninger and his colleagues recommended the elimination of terms like \"insane\" because they believe psychiatrists lack the authority or experience to pronounce on legal concepts such as \"responsibility,\" \"punishment,\" and \"justice.\" This implies that the term has legal implications that psychiatrists are not qualified to address.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (2): Psychiatry is concerned with treatment rather than aiding the legal profession. This is incorrect because while Menninger emphasises the treatment aspect of psychiatry, his argument against the term \"insane\" specifically focuses on the inappropriate legal implications of the term rather than a broader focus on treatment. Option (3): There is no clear definition of 'insane' that can be consistently applied. This is incorrect because the passage does not suggest that Menninger's argument relies on the inconsistency of the definition of \"insane.\" Instead, his argument is more about the inappropriate legal implications and the role of psychiatrists. Option (4): People labelled as 'insane' are often subjected to unjust punishments. This is incorrect because Menninger's argument against the use of the term \"insane\" is not directly based on the unjust punishments that might follow such labelling. While he is concerned about punishment and treatment, his specific argument about the term \"insane\" is rooted in the legal implications and the inappropriate role of psychiatrists in making legal judgments."}, {"n": 14, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "\"But playing God in this way has no conceivable moral or scientific justification.\" By stating this, Menninger intends to convey that:", "options": ["Determining the fate of a criminal through capital punishment is an act that should be reserved for a divine power.", "Society's reliance on capital punishment reflects a misunderstanding of morality and the science of criminal behaviour.", "The act of deciding to end someone's life is beyond human authority and cannot be justified by ethical or empirical standards.", "The imposition of the death penalty by a society is morally reprehensible and unsupported by scientific justifications."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 51, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation \"But playing God in this way has no conceivable moral or scientific justification.\" By stating this, Menninger intends to convey that: This question asks for an interpretation of Menninger's statement that \"playing God in this way has no conceivable moral or scientific justification.\" The correct answer should reflect Menninger's view on the moral and scientific aspects of capital punishment.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (3): The act of deciding to end someone's life is beyond human authority and cannot be justified by ethical or empirical standards. This is the correct answer. Menninger's use of the phrase \"playing God\" suggests that humans do not have the authority to make life-and-death decisions. By stating there is no moral or scientific justification, he indicates that neither ethical nor empirical standards can support such actions, meaning that humans should not assume the role of deciding to end a life.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (1): Determining the fate of a criminal through capital punishment is an act that should be reserved for a divine power. This is incorrect because Menninger's argument is not about reserving such decisions for a divine power, but rather that humans lack the moral and scientific basis to justify capital punishment. His focus is on the lack of justification, not on who should make such decisions. Option (2): Society's reliance on capital punishment reflects a misunderstanding of morality and the science of criminal behaviour. This is incorrect because while Menninger does criticise society's approach, this option does not fully capture the idea of \"playing God\" and the overstepping of human authority. It also does not explicitly address the concept of ending someone's life being beyond human authority. Option (4): The imposition of the death penalty by a society is morally reprehensible and unsupported by scientific justifications. This is partially correct but not as precise as Option (3). While Menninger does believe that capital punishment is morally wrong and lacks scientific justification, the phrase \"playing God\" specifically emphasises the idea of overstepping human authority in deciding life and death."}, {"n": 15, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "According to Menniger, both consequentialist and retributivist justifications of capital punishment fail because:", "options": ["the former assumes that there are no bad consequences of the punishment; the latter fails to recognise that there is no crime that deserves the killing of the guilty.", "the former fails to consider the possibility that the bad can outweigh the good for those involved; the latter advocates for inherently cruel and unjustifiable punishment.", "the former is flawed due to relying on consequences as a means of punishment; the latter denies those who seek justice the retribution that is necessary for such a crime.", "the former assumes that the consequences of the punishment lead to satisfaction for those involved; the latter assumes that the punishment will act as a deterrent."], "correct": "B", "cl_accuracy": 48, "solution": "Correct Answer – (2)\nQuestion Explanation According to Menniger, both consequentialist and retributivist justifications of capital punishment fail because: This question requires understanding Menninger's critique of both consequentialist and retributivist justifications for capital punishment as presented in the passage.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (2): the former fails to consider the possibility that the bad can outweigh the good for those involved; the latter advocates for inherently cruel and unjustifiable punishment. This is the correct answer. Menninger argues that from a consequentialist perspective, capital punishment cannot be justified because it does not result in a balance of good over bad consequences for those involved. He also argues that from a retributivist perspective, no crime can render the criminal deserving of the death penalty, implying that it is inherently cruel and unjustifiable.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (1): the former assumes that there are no bad consequences of the punishment; the latter fails to recognise that there is no crime that deserves the killing of the guilty. This is incorrect because it partially captures the retributivist perspective but misrepresents the consequentialist argument. Menninger's critique of the consequentialist justification is that it fails to consider that the bad consequences can outweigh the good, not that it assumes there are no bad consequences at all. Option (3): the former is flawed due to relying on consequences as a means of punishment; the latter denies those who seek justice the retribution that is necessary for such a crime. This is incorrect because it misrepresents both perspectives. The consequentialist critique is not about the reliance on consequences as a means of punishment but about the balance of good and bad consequences. The retributivist critique is that no crime deserves the death penalty, not that it denies justice-seekers the necessary retribution. Option (4): the former assumes that the consequences of the punishment lead to satisfaction for those involved; the latter assumes that the punishment will act as a deterrent. This is incorrect because it does not accurately reflect Menninger's arguments. The consequentialist critique is about the overall balance of consequences, not about satisfaction. The retributivist critique is about the inherent cruelty and lack of justification for the death penalty, not about its deterrent effect."}, {"n": 16, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "Which of the following is most likely the recommendation of Menninger for someone who committed murder?", "options": ["Avoid classifying them on one side or the other of a hypothetical line of so-called 'sanity'.", "Create a rehabilitation program aimed at treating the underlying behavioural disorder.", "Community service and restorative justice programs to help reintegrate them into society.", "Sentence them to life imprisonment to ensure both public safety and avoidance of unjustifiable punishments."], "correct": "B", "cl_accuracy": 74, "solution": "Correct Answer – (2)\nQuestion Explanation Which of the following is most likely the recommendation of Menninger for someone who committed murder? This question asks for the recommendation that Menninger would most likely make for someone who has committed murder, based on his views presented in the passage.\nCorrect Answer Explanation Option (2): Create a rehabilitation program aimed at treating the underlying behavioural disorder. This is the correct answer. Menninger believes that criminal behaviour should be treated similarly to behavioural disorders and mental illnesses, focusing on treatment rather than punishment. The passage states that Menninger argues against all forms of punishment, including capital punishment, and advocates for treating criminal behaviour.\nTherefore, creating a rehabilitation program to address the underlying behavioural disorder aligns with his views.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations Option (1): Avoid classifying them on one side or the other of a hypothetical line of so-called ‘sanity’. This is incorrect because while Menninger opposes the use of terms like \"insane,\" this option does not directly address the recommendation for dealing with someone who committed murder. It focuses more on the classification issue rather than a treatment or rehabilitation approach. Option (3): Community service and restorative justice programs to help reintegrate them into society. This is incorrect because, although Menninger might support reintegration, the passage emphasises his focus on treatment of underlying behavioural disorders rather than specific restorative justice programs. Option (4): Sentence them to life imprisonment to ensure both public safety and avoidance of unjustifiable punishments. This is incorrect because Menninger is against punishment in general. Life imprisonment, although avoiding capital punishment, is still a form of punishment rather than treatment. Menninger advocates for treating criminal behavior, not for punitive measures."}, {"n": 17, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "There is a sentence that is missing in the paragraph below. Look at the paragraph and decide where (option 1, 2, 3, or 4) the following sentence would best fit.\n\nSentence: So diverse, in fact, are the meanings of silence that one sociologist has protested that silence is not a natural unit in the structure of face-to-face interaction at all.\n\nParagraph: If one starts to think of what silences may mean to people, he is immediately struck by a baffling variety of meanings.__1__. In many cases, silence is really the equivalent of something else. It is doubtful, therefore, whether an analysis of silences as such can be a very fruitful endeavour. __2__. Granted the fact of diversity, there is a feature common to social silences that makes them a distinct class of events. __3__. Silences have a paradoxical character, a character that provides a basis for classifying them. __4__. A consideration of this classification, in turn, will reveal limitations in one possible approach to silence, which is predominant in sociology today, while another approach, that of G. H. Mead, is a minority position, yet probably the more helpful of the two for the analysis of silence.", "options": ["Option 4", "Option 3", "Option 1", "Option 2"], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 50, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nAnalysis of the Missing Sentence: \"So diverse, in fact, are the meanings of silence that one sociologist has protested that silence is not a natural unit in the structure of face-to-face interaction at all.\" This sentence highlights the vast range of meanings attributed to silence, to the extent that it challenges the idea of silence being a distinct component in social interactions. Now, let's evaluate each blank to find the best fit.\nEvaluation of Each Option Option 1: 1. Context: \"If one starts to think of what silences may mean to people, he is immediately struck by a baffling variety of meanings. Blank 1. In many cases, silence is really the equivalent of something else. It is doubtful, therefore, whether an analysis of silences as such can be a very fruitful endeavour.\" 2. Analysis: This blank directly follows the statement about the diverse meanings of silence. Placing the sentence here would reinforce and elaborate on the initial observation, emphasising the extent of this diversity with a specific example. 3. Fit: Strong. This position directly supports the preceding statement, making it a logical and seamless fit.\nOption 2:\n• Context: \"It is doubtful, therefore, whether an analysis of silences as such can be a very fruitful endeavour. Option 2. Granted the fact of diversity, there is a feature common to social silences that makes them a distinct class of events.\"\n• Analysis: This blank follows a statement about the potential futility of analysing silences. Inserting the sentence here would disrupt the flow of reasoning, as it would introduce a new idea without directly linking to the previous sentence.\n• Fit: Weak. It doesn't transition smoothly from the point about the futility of analysis to the diversity of meanings.\nOption 3:\n• Context: \"Granted the fact of diversity, there is a feature common to social silences that makes them a distinct class of events. Blank 3. Silences have a paradoxical character, a character that provides a basis for classifying them.\"\n• Analysis: This blank introduces a new point about a common feature of social silences. Inserting the sentence here would shift the focus back to the diversity of meanings, potentially confusing the reader and disrupting the flow.\n• Fit: Weak. It interrupts the transition to discussing common features of silences.\nOption 4:\n• Context: \"Silences have a paradoxical character, a character that provides a basis for classifying them. Blank 4. A consideration of this classification, in turn, will reveal limitations in one possible approach to silence, which is predominant in sociology today, while another approach, that of G. H. Mead, is a minority position, yet probably the more helpful of the two for the analysis of silence.\"\n• Analysis: This blank follows a statement about the paradoxical nature of silences. Adding the sentence here would be redundant, as the paragraph already moves towards a deeper analysis of silence rather than reiterating its diversity.\n• Fit: Weak. This position would be redundant and disrupt the flow of the concluding observations.\nConclusion Based on the analysis, the best fit for the sentence \"So diverse, in fact, are the meanings of silence that one sociologist has protested that silence is not a natural unit in the structure of face-to-face interaction at all.\" is Blank 1. This position allows the sentence to elaborate on the initial statement, reinforcing the idea of the diversity of silence's meanings.\nRevised Paragraph: If one starts to think of what silences may mean to people, he is immediately struck by a baffling variety of meanings. So diverse, in fact, are the meanings of silence that one sociologist has protested that silence is not a natural unit in the structure of face-to-face interaction at all. In many cases, silence is really the equivalent of something else. It is doubtful, therefore, whether an analysis of silences as such can be a very fruitful endeavour. Granted the fact of diversity, there is a feature common to social silences that makes them a distinct class of events. Silences have a paradoxical character, a character that provides a basis for classifying them. A consideration of this classification, in turn, will reveal limitations in one possible approach to silence, which is predominant in sociology today, while another approach, that of G. H. Mead, is a minority position, yet probably the more helpful of the two for the analysis of silence."}, {"n": 18, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "There is a sentence that is missing in the paragraph below. Look at the paragraph and decide where (option 1, 2, 3, or 4) the following sentence would best fit.\n\nSentence: This duality enriches the literary form, much like Cubism enriches the visual arts, by challenging the reader to perceive and interpret multiple layers of meaning simultaneously.\n\nParagraph: Unless we adopt Wylie Sypher’s definition of Cubism in Rococo to Cubism in Art and Literature (1960) and find it in any art form studying its own processes without recourse to representational reality, we may question whether “Cubist” or “cubistic” are viable terms in literature. __1__. Even if we decide that such an epithet may appropriately be applied to the alternating tones of Max Jacob's poetic works, it is essential to recognise the inherent limitations and the necessity of contextual understanding. __2__. Jacob's writings, while exhibiting the fragmented and multifaceted nature akin to Cubist visual art, also delve deeply into the realms of personal and philosophical introspection. __3__. Moreover, applying ", "options": ["Option 3", "Option 1", "Option 4", "Option 2"], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 59, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nAnalysis of the Missing Sentence: \"This duality enriches the literary form, much like Cubism enriches the visual arts, by challenging the reader to perceive and interpret multiple layers of meaning simultaneously.\" This sentence emphasises how the duality in literature, akin to Cubism in visual arts, challenges readers to engage with multiple layers of meaning. Now, let's evaluate each blank to find the best fit.\nEvaluation of Each Option Option 1:\n• Context: \"Unless we adopt Wylie Sypher’s definition of Cubism in Rococo to Cubism in Art and Literature (1960) and find it in any art form studying its own processes without recourse to representational reality, we may question whether “Cubist” or “cubistic” are viable terms in literature. Blank 1. Even if we decide that such an epithet may appropriately be applied to the alternating tones of Max Jacob's poetic works, it is essential to recognise the inherent limitations and the necessity of contextual understanding.\"\n• Analysis: This blank follows a sentence questioning the application of Cubist terms in literature. Inserting the sentence here would disrupt the logical flow from questioning the terms to discussing specific examples.\n• Fit: Weak. It doesn't provide a smooth transition to the next part about Max Jacob's works.\nOption 2:\n• Context: \"Even if we decide that such an epithet may appropriately be applied to the alternating tones of Max Jacob's poetic works, it is essential to recognise the inherent limitations and the necessity of contextual understanding. 2. Jacob's writings, while exhibiting the fragmented and multifaceted nature akin to Cubist visual art, also delve deeply into the realms of personal and philosophical introspection.\"\n• Analysis: This blank comes after acknowledging the potential application of the term to Max Jacob's works. Inserting the sentence here would disrupt the flow from acknowledging the term to discussing the nature of Jacob's writings.\n• Fit: Weak. It doesn't provide a smooth transition to discussing Jacob's works.\nOption 3:\n• Context: \"Jacob's writings, while exhibiting the fragmented and multifaceted nature akin to Cubist visual art, also delve deeply into the realms of personal and philosophical introspection. Blank 3. Moreover, applying \"Cubist\" as a descriptor in literature encourages a re-evaluation of traditional narrative structures.\"\n• Analysis: This blank follows a sentence describing Jacob's writings. Inserting the sentence here would reinforce the description of the duality (fragmented and multifaceted nature) in Jacob's writings, transitioning smoothly to the broader discussion of Cubism in literature.\n• Fit: Strong. It fits well here, reinforcing the description of Jacob's writings and leading into the broader implications. The dichotomy refers to the coexistence of fragmented structure and deep introspection in Jacob's works.\nOption 4:\n• Context: \"Moreover, applying \"Cubist\" as a descriptor in literature encourages a re-evaluation of traditional narrative structures. Blank 4. Their works, much like the Cubist paintings of Picasso, invite readers to engage with a text that defies straightforward interpretation, instead offering a composite view of reality from diverse perspectives.\"\n• Analysis: This blank follows a discussion of re-evaluating narrative structures. Inserting the sentence here would be somewhat redundant and disrupt the flow to the concluding observations.\n• Fit: Weak. This position would be redundant and disrupt the flow of the concluding observations.\nConclusion Based on the analysis, the best fit for the sentence \"This duality enriches the literary form, much like Cubism enriches the visual arts, by challenging the reader to perceive and interpret multiple layers of meaning simultaneously.\" is Blank 3. This position allows the sentence to reinforce the description of Jacob's writings and transition smoothly to the broader implications of Cubism in literature.\nRevised Paragraph: Unless we adopt Wylie Sypher’s definition of Cubism in Rococo to Cubism in Art and Literature (1960) and find it in any art form studying its own processes without recourse to representational reality, we may question whether “Cubist” or “cubistic” are viable terms in literature. Even if we decide that such an epithet may appropriately be applied to the alternating tones of Max Jacob's poetic works, it is essential to recognise the inherent limitations and the necessity of contextual understanding. Jacob's writings, while exhibiting the fragmented and multifaceted nature akin to Cubist visual art, also delve deeply into the realms of personal and philosophical introspection. This duality enriches the literary form, much like Cubism enriches the visual arts, by challenging the reader to perceive and interpret multiple layers of meaning simultaneously. Moreover, applying \"Cubist\" as a descriptor in literature encourages a re-evaluation of traditional narrative structures. Authors like Joyce, with his stream-of-consciousness technique in Ulysses, and Gide, with his nonlinear storytelling in The Counterfeiters, exemplify this shift. Their works, much like the Cubist paintings of Picasso, invite readers to engage with a text that defies straightforward interpretation, instead offering a composite view of reality from diverse perspectives."}, {"n": 19, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "Five jumbled up sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5), related to a topic, are given below. Four of them can be put together to form a coherent paragraph. Identify the odd sentence and key in the number of that sentence as your answer.\n\n1. His wife told him that one of the things she will miss most is being able to come to him, ask him a question, and he will sit there and calmly explain the world to her.\n2. Bommer said yes, and his wife was all in, too.\n3. He posted online, it was time to say goodbye.\n4. Bommer was diagnosed with colon cancer, and the doctors told him it was terminal.\n5. His friend called him up, saying he had an opportunity at his company for him to build an interactive AI version of Bommer.", "options": [], "correct": "1", "cl_accuracy": 31, "solution": "Correct Answer – 1\nAnalysis of Sentences:\nSentence 1: \"His wife told him that one of the things she will miss most is being able to come to him, ask him a question, and he will sit there and calmly explain the world to her.\" This sentence introduces a personal and emotional element, reflecting the wife’s feelings about Bommer.\nSentence 2: \"Bommer said yes, and his wife was all in, too.\" This sentence shows a response, indicating agreement and support from Bommer and his wife.\nSentence 3: \"He posted online, it was time to say goodbye.\" This sentence conveys Bommer’s public announcement about his condition and his readiness to part ways.\nSentence 4: \"Bommer was diagnosed with colon cancer, and the doctors told him it was terminal.\" This sentence provides the critical context of Bommer’s diagnosis, which sets the stage for the subsequent events.\nSentence 5: \"His friend called him up, saying he had an opportunity at his company for him to build an interactive AI version of Bommer.\" This sentence introduces a new opportunity presented to Bommer by his friend, which is related to his situation.\nCoherent Paragraph Construction (Order 4352):\n• Sentence 4: \"Bommer was diagnosed with colon cancer, and the doctors told him it was terminal.\" This sentence starts by providing the critical context of Bommer’s diagnosis, which sets the stage for the subsequent events.\n• Sentence 3: \"He posted online, it was time to say goodbye.\" Following the diagnosis, this sentence conveys Bommer’s public announcement about his condition and his readiness to part ways.\n• Sentence 5: \"His friend called him up, saying he had an opportunity at his company for him to build an interactive AI version of Bommer.\" This sentence introduces a new opportunity presented to Bommer by his friend, which is related to his situation.\n• Sentence 2: \"Bommer said yes, and his wife was all in, too.\" This sentence shows a response, indicating agreement and support from Bommer and his wife. These sentences together form a coherent narrative that starts with Bommer’s diagnosis, follows with his public announcement, introduces an opportunity from his friend, and concludes with his and his wife’s agreement to pursue it.\nOdd Sentence:\nSentence 1: \"His wife told him that one of the things she will miss most is being able to come to him, ask him a question, and he will sit there and calmly explain the world to her.\" This sentence, while related to Bommer’s situation, focuses specifically on the personal feelings of his wife and does not tie directly to the broader narrative about Bommer’s diagnosis, public announcement, and subsequent opportunity as effectively as the other sentences."}, {"n": 20, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "Five jumbled up sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5), related to a topic, are given below. Four of them can be put together to form a coherent paragraph. Identify the odd sentence and key in the number of that sentence as your answer.\n\n1. This duality makes it a unique subject of study in the broader context of logical fallacies.\n2. In fact, at least as a first approximation, one could reconstruct the past fifty years of this field largely as a series of footnotes to Hamblin's Fallacies and of developments culminating with Woods's Errors of Reasoning.\n3. From a theoretical point of view, the study of the fallacy of composition is a special case of a key and well-established branch of logic and argumentation theory.\n4. The fallacy of composition is special not only in the sense of being a specific case of fallacies but also in the sense of being especially important.\n5. There are both theoretical and practical motivations for wanting to study the fallacy of composition.", "options": [], "correct": "5", "cl_accuracy": 15, "solution": "Correct Answer – 5\nAnalysis of Sentences:\nSentence 1: \"This duality makes it a unique subject of study in the broader context of logical fallacies.\" This sentence emphasises the uniqueness of a subject due to its dual nature within logical fallacies.\nSentence 2: \"In fact, at least as a first approximation, one could reconstruct the past fifty years of this field largely as a series of footnotes to Hamblin's Fallacies and of developments culminating with Woods's Errors of Reasoning.\" This sentence provides a historical overview, highlighting the importance of works by Hamblin and Woods in the field of logical fallacies.\nSentence 3: \"From a theoretical point of view, the study of the fallacy of composition is a special case of a key and well-established branch of logic and argumentation theory.\" This sentence introduces the fallacy of composition, placing it within the broader context of logic and argumentation theory.\nSentence 4: \"The fallacy of composition is special not only in the sense of being a specific case of fallacies but also in the sense of being especially important.\" This sentence elaborates on the significance of the fallacy of composition, mentioning its special and important nature.\nSentence 5: \"There are both theoretical and practical motivations for wanting to study the fallacy of composition.\" This sentence discusses the motivations for studying the fallacy of composition, both theoretical and practical.\nCoherent Paragraph Construction (Order 3241):\n• Sentence 3: \"From a theoretical point of view, the study of the fallacy of composition is a special case of a key and well-established branch of logic and argumentation theory.\" This sentence introduces the fallacy of composition within the broader context of logic and argumentation theory.\n• Sentence 2: \"In fact, at least as a first approximation, one could reconstruct the past fifty years of this field largely as a series of footnotes to Hamblin's Fallacies and of developments culminating with Woods's Errors of Reasoning.\" Following the introduction, this sentence provides a historical overview of the field.\n• Sentence 4: \"The fallacy of composition is special not only in the sense of being a specific case of fallacies but also in the sense of being especially important.\" This sentence emphasises the special and important nature of the fallacy of composition.\n• Sentence 1: \"This duality makes it a unique subject of study in the broader context of logical fallacies.\" This sentence highlights the duality that makes the fallacy of composition a unique subject of study. These sentences together form a coherent narrative that starts with an introduction to the fallacy of composition, provides historical context, emphasises its significance, and highlights its uniqueness.\nOdd Sentence:\nSentence 5: \"There are both theoretical and practical motivations for wanting to study the fallacy of composition.\" Sentence 5, while related, shifts the focus to the motivations for studying the fallacy, which is a different angle compared to the other sentences. It introduces practical motivations, which don't fit as seamlessly into the theoretical and historical discussion formed by the other sentences."}, {"n": 21, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The four sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3 and 4) given below, when properly sequenced, would yield a coherent paragraph. Decide on the proper sequencing of the order of the sentences and key in the sequence of the four numbers as your answer.\n\n1. Whether the past can ever be truly laid to rest is a contentious issue.\n2. We should not commit the mistake philosopher George Santayana once warned, “Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it”.\n3. But only the recognition of the past’s horror can lead to any meaningful end to the anger that it had caused.\n4. We should continue to read and talk about the Civil War and condemn the brutal acts of murder and repression that have affected Spain for at least three generations.", "options": [], "correct": "1324", "cl_accuracy": 16, "solution": "Correct Answer – 1324\nAnalysis of Each Sentence Sentence 1: \"Whether the past can ever be truly laid to rest is a contentious issue.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"contentious issue\" indicates a debate or argument.\n• Antecedent requirement: None; this sentence broadly introduces the main topic of debate.\n• Broad/Narrow: This is a broad statement setting the context for the entire discussion.\nSentence 2: \"We should not commit the mistake philosopher George Santayana once warned, 'Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it'.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"philosopher George Santayana\" and the quote serve as a warning.\n• Antecedent requirement: Needs context of the importance of remembering the past, which can follow a discussion of the consequences of the past.\n• Broad/Narrow: This is a narrow statement providing specific philosophical advice related to the topic.\nSentence 3: \"But only the recognition of the past’s horror can lead to any meaningful end to the anger that it had caused.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"But only\" indicates a condition or contrast.\n• Antecedent requirement: Needs context about the past's impact, such as horror or anger caused by it.\n• Broad/Narrow: This is a narrow statement addressing the consequences and resolution of past horrors.\nSentence 4: \"We should continue to read and talk about the Civil War and condemn the brutal acts of murder and repression that have affected Spain for at least three generations.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"We should continue\" implies an ongoing action.\n• Antecedent requirement: Needs context about the importance of remembering and discussing past events.\n• Broad/Narrow: This is a narrow statement providing a specific example and recommended actions.\nLogical Sequencing Sentence 1: \"Whether the past can ever be truly laid to rest is a contentious issue.\" Reasoning: This sets the stage by presenting the contentious issue of whether the past can be laid to rest.\nSentence 3: \"But only the recognition of the past’s horror can lead to any meaningful end to the anger that it had caused.\" Reasoning: This continues the discussion by stating that only by recognising the past's horrors can we find resolution. It directly addresses the impact of the past introduced in Sentence 1.\nSentence 2: \"We should not commit the mistake philosopher George Santayana once warned, 'Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it'.\" Reasoning: This introduces a philosophical perspective on the importance of remembering the past, reinforcing the significance of the issue raised in Sentence 1 and elaborated in Sentence 3.\nSentence 4: \"We should continue to read and talk about the Civil War and condemn the brutal acts of murder and repression that have affected Spain for at least three generations.\" Reasoning: This provides a specific example and a recommended action, demonstrating the practical application of the idea introduced in Sentences 1, 3, and 2.\nCoherent Paragraph By following the sequence 1-3-2-4, the paragraph flows logically from introducing the issue, discussing the necessity of recognising past horrors, presenting a philosophical warning, and providing a specific example and action. Here’s how it looks when combined: Whether the past can ever be truly laid to rest is a contentious issue. But only the recognition of the past’s horror can lead to any meaningful end to the anger that it had caused. We should not commit the mistake philosopher George Santayana once warned, 'Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it'. We should continue to read and talk about the Civil War and condemn the brutal acts of murder and repression that have affected Spain for at least three generations."}, {"n": 22, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The four sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3 and 4) given below, when properly sequenced, would yield a coherent paragraph. Decide on the proper sequencing of the order of the sentences and key in the sequence of the four numbers as your answer.\n\n1. But the allure of populism is strong.\n2. There is no space for nuance or complexity, and any hint of compromise is seen as betrayal.\n3. Politicians from either end of the political spectrum promise simple solutions to complex problems.\n4. The values around which centrism is built are the importance of moderation, pragmatism and compromise; an embrace of complexity; a commitment to liberal democracy; the belief in equality of opportunity; and the belief that through balancing tensions people’s lives can be made better.", "options": [], "correct": "4132", "cl_accuracy": 22, "solution": "Correct Answer – 4132\nAnalysis of Each Sentence Sentence 1: \"But the allure of populism is strong.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"But\" indicates contrast.\n• Antecedent requirement: Needs a context that contrasts with the allure of populism.\n• Broad/Narrow: Narrow, focusing on the specific appeal of populism.\nSentence 2: \"There is no space for nuance or complexity, and any hint of compromise is seen as betrayal.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"no space for nuance or complexity\" and \"compromise is seen as betrayal\" indicate a critique.\n• Antecedent requirement: Needs context about the nature of political discourse or actions.\n• Broad/Narrow: Narrow, focusing on the characteristics of populist politics.\nSentence 3: \"Politicians from either end of the political spectrum promise simple solutions to complex problems.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"Politicians from either end\" implies a general statement about political behaviour.\n• Antecedent requirement: None; this can introduce the issue of simplified solutions.\n• Broad/Narrow: Broad, addressing a general trend in politics.\nSentence 4: \"The values around which centrism is built are the importance of moderation, pragmatism and compromise; an embrace of complexity; a commitment to liberal democracy; the belief in equality of opportunity; and the belief that through balancing tensions people’s lives can be made better.\" Clues:\n• Signal words: \"The values around which centrism is built\" introduces the principles of centrism.\n• Antecedent requirement: None; this sentence broadly introduces the values of centrism.\n• Broad/Narrow: Broad, providing a detailed description of centrism.\nLogical Sequencing Sentence 4: \"The values around which centrism is built are the importance of moderation, pragmatism and compromise; an embrace of complexity; a commitment to liberal democracy; the belief in equality of opportunity; and the belief that through balancing tensions people’s lives can be made better.\" Reasoning: This sentence sets the stage by introducing the values of centrism, which contrasts with populism.\nSentence 1: \"But the allure of populism is strong.\" Reasoning: This sentence follows naturally, contrasting the values of centrism with the appeal of populism.\nSentence 3: \"Politicians from either end of the political spectrum promise simple solutions to complex problems.\" Reasoning: This sentence explains a common trait among politicians that feeds into the allure of populism.\nSentence 2: \"There is no space for nuance or complexity, and any hint of compromise is seen as betrayal.\" Reasoning: This sentence concludes by describing the consequences of populism, where nuance and compromise are rejected.\nCoherent Paragraph By following the sequence 4-1-3-2, the paragraph flows logically from introducing centrism, contrasting it with populism, describing political oversimplification, and concluding with the rejection of complexity and compromise. Here’s how it looks when combined: The values around which centrism is built are the importance of moderation, pragmatism and compromise; an embrace of complexity; a commitment to liberal democracy; the belief in equality of opportunity; and the belief that through balancing tensions, people’s lives can be made better. But the allure of populism is strong. Politicians from either end of the political spectrum promise simple solutions to complex problems. There is no space for nuance or complexity, and any hint of compromise is seen as betrayal."}, {"n": 23, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The passage given below is followed by four alternate summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage.\n\nIn recent years, a neutral theory of community organisation has been developed by scientists such as Steve Hubbell and Graham Bell, which assumes that all individuals within broadly defined groups (e.g., trees competing for space in a tropical forest) have equal probabilities of giving birth, dying, and dispersing to new locales, irrespective of taxonomic identity. This assumption seems biologically implausible, yet the neutral theory performs surprisingly well in predicting many patterns in species-rich communities. Neutrality does not predict perpetual coexistence, but instead transient coexistence as species drift in abundance, some increasing and others decreasing due to chance. In a closed neutral community, in the long run, just one species persists. Given immigration, species richness can be maintained if external source pools harbour multiple species. Yet, at a sufficiently large scale, there can be no immigration, and maintenance of diversity in this theory ultimately requires recurrent speciation.", "options": ["The neutral theory posits equal probabilities of birth, death, and dispersal among individuals within groups, predicting temporary coexistence and long-term species richness maintained by immigration and recurrent speciation.", "Despite seeming implausible, the neutral theory predicts species patterns by assuming equal probabilities of survival and reproduction chances, leading to continual coexistence with long-term diversity reliant on immigration and speciation.", "The neutral theory, assuming equal survival and reproduction for species, effectively predicts species coexistence transiently and highlights the necessity of immigration and recurrent speciation for maintaining long-term diversity.", "The neutral theory assumes equal birth, death, and dispersal probabilities for individuals, accurately predicting transient species coexistence with no large-scale immigration and speciation to maintain diversity over time."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 23, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nThe Passage: The main idea of the passage is that the neutral theory of community organisation, despite its biologically implausible assumption of functional equivalence among species, is surprisingly effective in predicting patterns in species-rich communities. While it does not predict perpetual coexistence, it suggests that species diversity can be maintained through immigration or recurrent speciation.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 1: The neutral theory posits equal probabilities of birth, death, and dispersal among individuals within groups, predicting temporary coexistence and long-term species richness maintained by immigration and recurrent speciation. This option is correct because it accurately summarises the key points of the passage. It mentions the core assumption of the neutral theory—equal probabilities of birth, death, and dispersal. It correctly notes that the theory predicts temporary coexistence of species and highlights the role of immigration and recurrent speciation in maintaining long-term species richness.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 2: Despite seeming implausible, the neutral theory predicts species patterns by assuming equal probabilities of survival and reproduction chances, leading to continual coexistence with long-term diversity reliant on immigration and speciation.\n• Issue 1: This option incorrectly states that the neutral theory predicts \"continual coexistence\" of species. The passage clearly mentions \"transient coexistence,\" meaning species coexist temporarily, not continuously.\n• Issue 2: It mentions \"survival and reproduction chances\" instead of \"birth, death, and dispersal probabilities,\" which is less precise and deviates from the specific terms used in the passage.\n• Issue 3: While it acknowledges the role of immigration and speciation, it does not clearly explain their necessity in the context of the theory.\nOption 3: The neutral theory, assuming equal survival and reproduction for species, effectively predicts species coexistence transiently and highlights the necessity of immigration and recurrent speciation for maintaining long-term diversity.\n• Issue 1: This option inaccurately states that the theory assumes \"equal survival and reproduction for species\" instead of individuals within groups, which misrepresents the theory's assumption.\n• Issue 2: It correctly notes transient coexistence but lacks clarity in conveying how immigration and recurrent speciation maintain long-term diversity. The explanation should emphasise the critical role of these factors more clearly.\nOption 4: The neutral theory assumes equal birth, death, and dispersal probabilities for individuals, accurately predicting transient species coexistence with no large-scale immigration and speciation to maintain diversity over time.\n• Issue 1: This option accurately describes the theory's assumptions and the concept of transient coexistence but incorrectly states \"no large-scale immigration and speciation.\" The passage specifies that long-term diversity is maintained through immigration from external sources and recurrent speciation.\n• Issue 2: The phrase \"with no large-scale immigration and speciation\" contradicts the passage's explanation of how diversity is maintained, making this option misleading."}, {"n": 24, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The passage given below is followed by four alternate summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage.\n\nAs things are, we notice that some people do consider others less than most of us, and we use words like selfish or mean to record this fact. Selfishness cannot, then, be a universal condition. This was a central problem for Hobbes, who went to great trouble to prove that, in all our acts, our real aim is always our own interest. To explain the appearance of disinterested behaviour, he focussed on enlightened self-interest, explaining that what looks like altruism is really only a shrewd insurance policy. Hobbes's trouble here was that he was not actually an immoralist. He did not actually want to get rid of the virtues. He liked an orderly society. He just wanted to stop people from using morality against their own interests, especially in futile revolts or religious wars. So he argued that virtue does serve for self-preservation, so long as the State is still functioning efficiently.", "options": ["Hobbes believed that selfishness was not universal, a view that clashed with his emphasis on self-interest as the primary motivator.", "Hobbes argued that even virtuous acts ultimately stem from self-interest, but this does not negate their value for social order.", "Hobbes's liking for an orderly society led him to promote the idea of selfishness as a means of self-preservation.", "Hobbes viewed altruism as a means to prevent selfishness, emphasising the need for an efficient State to protect the virtues."], "correct": "B", "cl_accuracy": 55, "solution": "Correct Answer – (2)\nThe Passage: The passage discusses Hobbes's view on selfishness and morality. Hobbes believed that all actions, including those appearing altruistic, are driven by self-interest, which he termed enlightened self-interest. His goal was not to eliminate virtues but to align them with self-preservation and societal order, particularly to prevent people from using morality to harm their own interests.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 2: Hobbes argued that even virtuous acts ultimately stem from self-interest, but this does not negate their value for social order. This option captures the essence of the passage. It correctly reflects Hobbes's belief that all acts, even virtuous ones, are driven by self-interest. It also acknowledges that Hobbes did not seek to negate the value of virtues but rather to integrate them into a framework that supports social order.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: Hobbes believed that selfishness was not universal, a view that clashed with his emphasis on self-interest as the primary motivator. Issue: This option misinterprets the passage. Hobbes did believe in universal self-interest but struggled with the appearance of disinterested behaviour. The passage does not suggest that selfishness was not universal, but rather that Hobbes aimed to explain all actions through self-interest.\nOption 3: Hobbes's liking for an orderly society led him to promote the idea of selfishness as a means of self-preservation. Issue: This option oversimplifies Hobbes's argument. While Hobbes did value social order and saw self-preservation as important, his focus was on enlightened self-interest as the underlying motivator for all actions, not just selfishness as a means of self-preservation.\nOption 4: Hobbes viewed altruism as a means to prevent selfishness, emphasising the need for an efficient State to protect the virtues. Issue: This option inaccurately represents Hobbes's view. He did not see altruism as a means to prevent selfishness; rather, he argued that what appears to be altruism is actually enlightened self-interest. Additionally, Hobbes emphasised the need for an efficient State to align virtues with self-preservation, not merely to protect virtues."}]}, "s2": {"id": "s2", "title": "VARC Sectional 2", "source": "VARC 02 - 2026", "durationSec": 2400, "marking": {"MCQ": [3, -1], "TITA": [3, 0]}, "passages": {"P1": "Easterlin, in his original study, revealed a paradox that sparked interest in the topic but is, as of yet, unresolved. While most happiness studies find that within countries, wealthier people are, on average, happier than poor ones, studies across countries and over time find very little, if any, relationship between increases in per capita income and average happiness levels. On average, wealthier countries (as a group) are happier than poor ones (as a group); happiness seems to rise with income up to a point, but not beyond it. Yet even among the less happy, poorer countries, there is not a clear relationship between average income and average happiness levels, suggesting that many other factors—including cultural traits—are at play.\n\nWithin countries, income matters to happiness. Deprivation and abject poverty, in particular, are very bad for happiness. Yet after basic needs are met, other factors such as rising aspirations, relative income differences, and the security of gains become increasingly important, in addition to income. Long before the economics of happiness was established, James Duesenberry noted the role of changing aspirations on income satisfaction and its potential effects on consumption and savings rates. Any number of happiness studies have since confirmed the effects of rising aspirations and have also noted their potential role in driving excessive consumption and other perverse economic behaviours. . . .\n\nA common interpretation of the Easterlin paradox is that humans are on a “hedonic treadmill”: aspirations increase along with income, and after basic needs are met, relative rather than absolute levels of income matter to well-being. Another interpretation of the paradox is the psychologists’ “set point” theory of happiness, in which every individual is presumed to have a happiness level that he or she goes back to over time, even after major events such as winning the lottery or getting divorced. In this case, the implications for policy are that nothing much can be done to increase happiness.\n\nIndividuals are remarkably adaptable, no doubt, and in the end, can get used to most things, in particular to income gains. The behavioural economics literature, for example, shows that individuals value losses disproportionately over gains. Easterlin argues that individuals adapt more in the pecuniary arena than in the non-pecuniary arena, while life-changing events, such as bereavement, have lasting effects on happiness. Yet because most policy is based on pecuniary measures of well-being, it over-emphasises the importance of income gains to well-being and underestimates that of other factors, such as health, family, and stable employment.\n\nThere is no consensus about which interpretation is most accurate. Yet numerous studies, which demonstrate that happiness levels can change significantly in response to a variety of factors, suggest that the research can yield insights into human well-being, which provide important, if complementary, information for policymakers. Even under the rubric of set point theory, happiness levels can fall significantly in the aftermath of events like illness or unemployment. Even if levels eventually adapt upwards to a longer-term equilibrium, mitigating or preventing the unhappiness and disruption that individuals experience for months, or even years, in the interim certainly seems like a worthwhile objective for policy.", "P2": "Drawing upon data from a study examining experiences of accessing support for pets from the UK animal welfare charity Blue Cross, this paper illuminates reasons why people might not seek support when they need it. . . . Twenty Blue Cross service users took part in individual online interviews using a semi-guided narrative approach, where they were encouraged to share their experiences of reaching out. . . . Three shared narratives came through strongly in the interviews that show how wider societal attitudes and values shape identities and behaviours. These related to ‘giving back’, ‘sacrifice’ and the notion of ‘equity’.\n\n‘Giving back’ denotes a wider cultural belief that you give back what you take out, the moral obligation to return the favour. The desire to ‘give back’ was evident in many participants’ narratives, usually with respect to the donation given to Blue Cross for pet treatment, but people also talked about taking on rescue animals as a form of ‘giving back’, donating things for animal welfare charities to sell in their shops, taking part in the charity lottery, or. . .volunteering their time. . . . The amount or frequency of giving a donation was clearly uppermost in participants’ minds, alongside a strong desire to do the right thing. Emphasising how they did not just give the minimum or a small proportion of the cost if you were able to pay more, they also recognised that there may be times when [they] are not able to. . . .\n\nThe majority of participants demonstrated the strength of their bond with their pets and a commitment to them through the language of sacrifice, putting the animals’ needs first, always above their own. . . . Many participants described how changes in circumstances, often combined with the cost-of-living crisis, meant they had to economise. . . . This ‘pulling back’ rarely affected the pet’s care because they ‘came first’, but it would affect the owner. . . .\n\nImportantly, with respect to understanding a reluctance to seek support, there was a common tendency for participants to question whether others should be eligible for financial support for their pets and compare themselves and the donations they made with others who do not appear to be doing the same. The result is some resentment towards those who appear to be ‘taking advantage’ and stretching the system of support. . . .\n\nAlthough there was recognition that people fall on hard times and should be helped when they really need it, there were clear concerns that a lot of people ‘take advantage’ (never making any donation at all, not prioritising their pet, or making any sacrifice on their part, and putting undue strain on the charity). There was a strong sense of the current situation being unfair, with some people making suggestions as to how this might be better managed (e.g. a basic monthly payment made by all recipients of support). In essence, this narrative links to being a good moral citizen, both within the pet-owning community and, more broadly – thinking about other pet owners and people in need.", "P3": "[The writer and philosopher Benedict Rattigan] presented an idea that we have fundamentally misunderstood symmetry, and that its reach is far more extensive than has previously been recognised. Rattigan's theory intrigued me, and we worked together to invite other leading Oxford professors specialising in multiple disciplines to explain in lay terms the role of symmetry in their fields. . . .\n\nWhat is the common theme running through the various \"languages\" of symmetry?... The concept of symmetry is used in significantly different ways. . ., but there is one sense of symmetry that stands out from the variety of forms: a fundamental symmetry is that between order and disorder. It may seem paradoxical to suggest that Nature's ordering principle encompasses a symmetry between order and… well, precisely disorder. . . . What we found as the talks were drafted was that each form of the language of symmetry revealed a new aspect of the order-disorder principle. . . .\n\nEmpirical evidence for the symmetry between order and disorder is hiding in plain sight all around us. In 1999, the Danish physicist Per Bak suggested to a group of neuroscientists that the brain works on the same fundamental principles as a sand-pile. . . . Bak observed that the sand-pile maintains order by means of these random and unpredictable avalanches, which is an example of disorder being \"harnessed\" by the sand-pile as a means of maintaining order. In other words, there is a fundamental interdependence between them. It has been demonstrated that this model can be applied to a wide variety of different phenomena, from financial markets and traffic flows to earthquakes, black holes, and the distribution of galaxies in the universe. . . .\n\nAnother example of order and disorder forming a symmetry can be found in that most random of phenomena, crowd behaviour. . . . Watching a group of people negotiating a bottleneck at the doors as they filed into a lecture theatre, I observed what I, as a layman, would describe as chaos. But any social scientist will tell us that the behaviour of a cluster of people conforms to broadly predictable patterns, patterns that are determined by factors such as geometric boundary conditions or time gap distributions. . . .\n\nBut why is symmetry so symmetrical? The conclusion we reached is this: because it conditions its own structure. Whereas all the laws of Nature are built upon certain symmetries, symmetry alone is subject to itself.\n\nIn Nature's laws, complexity never develops beyond necessity. The best designs are simple, and the best designer is Nature itself. Finding that there is a fundamental symmetry between order and disorder that can run through all our explanations is like discovering a clarification that was always waiting there to be revealed. Whereas this seems at first to be illogical, if symmetry were life's ordering principle, then it would necessarily have infinite reach and encompass everything, including itself. This would create a paradoxical universe of symmetry and asymmetry, of cosmos and chaos. Our universe, in other words — is a place of infinite symmetries in which the co-existence of order and disorder is not only in evidence, it is inevitable. If symmetry has no deeper cause than itself, then the cosmos has been structured in the only way possible.", "P4": "In the pulsating core of London, the Oriental Club, nestled just off Oxford Street, embodies an air of tranquil dominance, evoking vivid, nostalgic images of British hegemony during the Raj with its rich mahogany panelling, supple leather armchairs, and the persistent scent of mulligatawny. This evocative portrayal continues to sculpt the collective perception of British colonial rule, suffusing media portrayals and even the most stringent criticisms. Advocates and critics alike generally agree on the perceived operational efficiency of British governance, diverging primarily over ethical assessments.\n\nYet, this representation of systematic administration is a façade. Spanning from the mid-18th century until 1947, British India was rife with tumult, violence, unrestrained fervours, and deep-seated unease. The inception of British dominion was unintended; their original involvement commenced through the monopolistic ventures of the East India Company, propelled by mercantile ambitions rather than administrative intentions. Initial interactions were fraught with apprehensions concerning competition from European traders and indigenous sovereigns, which catalysed the militarisation of commerce and the construction of strategic fortifications and military garrisons. Driven by a blend of defensive paranoia and the lust for martial renown, the British embarked on a campaign of conquest from the mid-18th century, establishing fortified enclaves and dispensing the bare minimum in governance to perpetuate their dominion.\n\nThrough the 19th and early 20th centuries, British infrastructural endeavours were predominantly reactionary measures to emergent crises, rather than calculated strategies to uplift Indian society or advance British commercial interests. The initiation of irrigation projects in the 1850s was spurred by economic calamities that ignited fears of insurrection and diminishing tax revenues. Railway expansion gained momentum following the 1857 revolt, highlighting the urgent need for swift troop movements to quell uprisings. Even as British capitalists in the mid-19th century advocated for increased investments in India, colonial officials remained hesitant unless such expenditures directly bolstered their control. This oversight led to a famine-prone India by the late 19th century. British policies systematically dismantled traditional Indian safety nets during disasters, with famine relief operations focusing on maintaining British supremacy and curbing expenses through famine camps, thereby severing the afflicted from imperial urban centres.\n\nThe tenure of the British Raj culminated in a cycle of violence, fuelled by deep-rooted security paranoia. The world wars transformed India into a vital, self-sustaining military stronghold for Britain's Asian campaigns, intensifying imperial anxieties. The entrenched belief in Indian dominion became a core ideology among imperial families, rendering any suggestion of withdrawal tantamount to an existential crisis. This reached a zenith with catastrophic events like General Dyer's massacre at Amritsar's Jallianwala Bagh in 1919, which obliterated the hopes of Indian nationalists for peaceful negotiations. Figures such as Gandhi endeavoured to juxtapose British-induced chaos with visions of a harmonious, non-violent society. However, the global economic depression and ongoing conflicts fragmented Indian communities. The Raj's inability to safeguard diverse populations engendered fear and retaliatory violence throughout northern India. By the conclusion of World War Two, widespread poverty and social disintegration prevailed, and by 1946, even British nationals questioned their government's ability to maintain security. The hasty British exodus in 1947 highlighted this diminished confidence. For too long, the myths perpetuated by retired officers in enclaves like the Oriental Club have obscured reality."}, "passageExplanations": {"P1": "Passage Explanation for Q1-4:\nParagraph 1\n• Main Point: The Easterlin paradox reveals a complex relationship between income and happiness, which remains unresolved.\n• Details: While wealthier individuals within a country are generally happier, cross-country and longitudinal studies show little correlation between increased per capita income and average happiness levels. Wealthier countries tend to be happier up to a certain income point, but other factors, including cultural traits, influence happiness levels, especially in poorer countries.\nParagraph 2\n• Main Point: Income affects happiness significantly within countries, particularly in alleviating poverty, but other factors become important after basic needs are met.\n• Details: Deprivation is detrimental to happiness, but once basic needs are satisfied, factors like rising aspirations, relative income, and the security of gains become crucial. James Duesenberry's work highlighted how changing aspirations influence income satisfaction and economic behaviours. Subsequent studies confirm that rising aspirations can drive excessive consumption and other adverse economic behaviours.\nParagraph 3\n• Main Point: Two main interpretations of the Easterlin paradox exist: the “hedonic treadmill” and the “set point” theory of happiness.\n• Details: The “hedonic treadmill” suggests that increased aspirations keep pace with income, making relative income more important than absolute income for well-being. The “set point” theory posits that individuals return to a baseline happiness level after major events. This implies that policy might not significantly influence long-term happiness.\nParagraph 4\n• Main Point: Adaptability and the disproportionate value placed on losses over gains influence happiness, highlighting limitations in policies focused on income.\n• Details: Individuals adapt to income gains but value losses more heavily. Easterlin argues that adaptation is more pronounced in financial areas than in non-pecuniary ones, with life events like bereavement having lasting effects on happiness. Policies overly focused on income gains overlook the importance of factors like health, family, and employment stability for well-being.\nParagraph 5\n• Main Point: There is no consensus on the most accurate interpretation of the Easterlin paradox, but happiness research offers valuable insights for policymakers.\n• Details: Studies show that happiness levels can change significantly due to various factors, suggesting useful insights for policy. Even with the set point theory, happiness can drop significantly after events like illness or unemployment. Policies aimed at mitigating short-term unhappiness and disruption are worthwhile, even if long-term happiness levels eventually stabilise.\nSummary\nThe passage explores the Easterlin paradox, highlighting the unresolved relationship between income and happiness. Within countries, wealth correlates with happiness, but cross-country and longitudinal studies reveal a limited impact of per capita income increases on average happiness levels. Factors like rising aspirations, relative income, and security gains become crucial once basic needs are met. The paradox is interpreted through the “hedonic treadmill” and “set point” theories, suggesting the relative importance of income and adaptability. Despite the lack of consensus, happiness research provides insights that can inform policies focusing on short-term well-being, recognising the importance of non-pecuniary factors like health and family stability.", "P2": "Passage Explanation for Q5 to 8\nParagraph 1\n• Main Point: The paper explores why people might hesitate to seek support from the UK animal welfare charity Blue Cross.\n• Details: The study involves individual online interviews with twenty Blue Cross service users using a semi-guided narrative approach. Three prominent narratives emerged from the interviews, highlighting the influence of societal attitudes and values on individuals' behaviours and identities.\nParagraph 2\n• Main Point: The concept of 'giving back' reflects a cultural belief in reciprocating what one receives, perceived as a moral duty.\n• Details: Participants expressed a desire to 'give back' through donations to the Blue Cross, adopting rescue animals, contributing items for charity sales, participating in charity lotteries, or volunteering. The emphasis was on not just minimal donations but also the recognition of times when they might be unable to contribute financially.\nParagraph 3\n• Main Point: Participants prioritised their pets' needs above their own, demonstrating commitment through sacrifice.\n• Details: The bond between participants and their pets was evident in their willingness to economise due to changing circumstances and the cost-of-living crisis. Despite financial constraints, participants ensured their pets' needs were met first, often at their own expense.\nParagraph 4\n• Main Point: Participants expressed reluctance to seek support due to concerns about fairness and the potential for others to exploit the system.\n• Details: There was a tendency to question the eligibility of others for financial support, leading to resentment towards those perceived as taking advantage without making sacrifices or contributions. While there was sympathy for those in genuine need, participants were concerned about the strain on the charity and suggested measures like a basic monthly payment for all support recipients to ensure fairness.\nSummary\nThe study, based on interviews with Blue Cross service users, reveals key reasons behind the reluctance to seek support, shaped by societal values of 'giving back,' sacrifice, and fairness. Participants felt a moral obligation to reciprocate the support received through donations and other means. They prioritised their pets' needs, often at personal expense, and were wary of others exploiting the system, suggesting structured contributions to ensure equitable support distribution.", "P3": "Passage Explanations for Q9 to 12:\nParagraph 1 — Main Point: Benedict Rattigan proposed that the concept of symmetry is more extensive and misunderstood than previously recognised. Details: This theory intrigued the author, leading them to collaborate with Oxford professors from various disciplines to explain the role of symmetry in lay terms.\nParagraph 2 — Main Point: The fundamental symmetry between order and disorder is a common theme across different applications of symmetry. Details: Despite the varied uses of symmetry in different fields, the principle that stands out is the balance between order and disorder. This principle revealed new aspects of symmetry's role in nature as the talks were drafted.\nParagraph 3 — Main Point: Empirical evidence shows the interdependence between order and disorder in various phenomena. Details: Danish physicist Per Bak's sand-pile model illustrates how disorder (avalanches) maintains order, showing their interdependence. This model applies to phenomena like financial markets, traffic flows, earthquakes, black holes, and galaxy distributions.\nParagraph 4 — Main Point: Crowd behaviour exemplifies the symmetry between order and disorder. Details: Observing people negotiating a bottleneck, what seems like chaos to a layperson follows predictable patterns, shaped by factors like geometric boundaries and time gaps, indicating an underlying order within apparent disorder.\nParagraph 5 — Main Point: Symmetry is self-conditioning and foundational to Nature's laws. Details: Symmetry conditions its own structure, unlike other natural laws. Nature's optimal designs are simple, showing that the fundamental symmetry between order and disorder clarifies the universe's structure, encompassing everything and creating a universe of infinite symmetries and paradoxes.\nParagraph 6 — Main Point: The universe inherently contains infinite symmetries, balancing order and disorder. Details: Symmetry's self-sustaining nature implies that the universe, structured with infinite symmetries, inevitably includes both order and disorder. This inherent structure explains the cosmos as the only possible configuration, highlighting the inevitability of the order-disorder coexistence.\nSummary: The passage explores Benedict Rattigan's theory that symmetry is more extensive and misunderstood than previously thought, focusing on the fundamental symmetry between order and disorder. Empirical examples, such as Per Bak's sand-pile model and crowd behaviour, illustrate the interdependence of order and disorder. Symmetry is unique in conditioning its own structure and is foundational to Nature's simple and optimal designs. This inherent symmetry explains the universe's structure, encompassing both order and disorder, and making their coexistence inevitable, portraying the cosmos as a place of infinite symmetries.", "P4": "Passage Explanation for Qs13-16:\nParagraph 1 • Main Point: The Oriental Club in London symbolises British colonial nostalgia and perceived governance efficiency. • Details: The club's setting, with rich mahogany, leather armchairs, and the scent of mulligatawny, evokes British dominance during the Raj. This nostalgic portrayal influences both media representations and critiques of British colonial rule, with common agreement on its operational efficiency, although ethical views vary.\nParagraph 2 • Main Point: British India, contrary to the façade of systematic administration, was marked by disorder and unintended governance. • Details: British control began through the East India Company's mercantile ambitions, not administrative plans. Early interactions involved competition fears, leading to militarised commerce and fortified establishments. Driven by paranoia and martial glory, the British extended their dominance through minimal governance and strategic fortifications.\nParagraph 3 • Main Point: British infrastructure in India was largely reactionary, addressing crises rather than improving society or commerce. • Details: Irrigation projects started due to economic crises in the 1850s, and railway expansions followed the 1857 revolt to enable troop movements. Colonial officials were reluctant to invest unless it secured control, leading to frequent famines. British famine relief efforts prioritised maintaining control and cutting costs, severing the affected from urban centres.\nParagraph 4 • Main Point: The British Raj's tenure ended in violence, driven by security paranoia and the inability to maintain order. • Details: During the world wars, India became a crucial military base, heightening imperial anxieties—incidents like the Jallianwala Bagh massacre in 1919 shattered peaceful negotiation hopes. Figures like Gandhi contrasted British chaos with non-violence, but economic depression and conflicts fragmented communities. By WWII's end, widespread poverty and social disintegration were evident, with doubts about maintaining security leading to a rapid British exit in 1947. Myths of colonial efficiency, perpetuated by places like the Oriental Club, obscure these realities.\nSummary: The passage critiques the romanticised view of British colonial rule, symbolised by places like the Oriental Club. While British governance is often seen as efficient, this view masks the chaotic, reactive, and violent nature of their rule in India. The British initially engaged in India for commercial reasons, militarising their presence out of paranoia and conquest desires. Infrastructure projects were crisis responses rather than developmental efforts, resulting in frequent famines and inadequate governance. The Raj's tenure ended in violence and disarray, driven by deep-seated security fears and inability to maintain order, culminating in a hasty British withdrawal in 1947. Myths of colonial efficiency obscure the harsh realities of British rule in India."}, "questions": [{"n": 1, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "Which of the following scenarios best illustrates the Easterlin Paradox as described in the passage?", "options": ["Over a decade, both Country X and Country Y experienced an increase in average income, with Country X's average income rising to ₹40,00,000 per year and Country Y's to ₹3,60,000 per year, yet their average happiness levels remain largely unchanged.", "Despite Country X’s overall higher average income compared to Country Y, individuals in both countries experience fluctuations in happiness due to factors like health, family, and job stability.", "Within Country X, individuals earning ₹10,00,000 per year report significantly higher levels of happiness than those earning ₹2,00,000 per year, while in Country Y, individuals earning ₹1,00,000 per year are happier than those earning ₹20,000 per year.", "Country X, with an average income of ₹30,00,000 per year and a generally high standard of living, has a higher average happiness level than Country Y, which has an average income of ₹3,00,000 per year and faces challenges in meeting basic needs."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 32, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation\nWhich of the following scenarios best illustrates the Easterlin Paradox as described in the passage?\nThis question asks us to identify which scenario best illustrates the Easterlin Paradox as described in the passage. The Easterlin Paradox highlights the lack of a clear relationship between increased per capita income and average happiness levels across countries over time, despite wealthier individuals within a country being happier than poorer individuals.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(1) Over a decade, both Country X and Country Y experienced an increase in average income, with Country X's average income rising to ₹40,00,000 per year and Country Y's to ₹3,60,000 per year, yet their average happiness levels remain largely unchanged.\nThis scenario directly illustrates the Easterlin Paradox. It shows that despite significant increases in average income for both countries, there is no corresponding increase in their average happiness levels. This aligns with the paradox's observation that while within countries wealthier individuals are happier, across countries and over time, increases in income do not necessarily correlate with increases in happiness.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(2) Despite Country X’s overall higher average income compared to Country Y, individuals in both countries experience fluctuations in happiness due to factors like health, family, and job stability.\nWhile this scenario mentions factors other than income affecting happiness, it does not illustrate the core aspect of the Easterlin Paradox, which is the lack of a clear relationship between increased average income and average happiness levels over time across countries.\n(3) Within Country X, individuals earning ₹10,00,000 per year report significantly higher levels of happiness than those earning ₹2,00,000 per year, while in Country Y, individuals earning ₹1,00,000 per year are happier than those earning ₹20,000 per year.\nThis scenario describes within-country differences in happiness levels based on income, which is consistent with part of the Easterlin Paradox. However, it does not address the paradox's key aspect regarding the lack of a relationship between increases in average income and average happiness levels over time across countries.\n(4) Country X, with an average income of ₹30,00,000 per year and a generally high standard of living, has a higher average happiness level than Country Y, which has an average income of ₹3,00,000 per year and faces challenges in meeting basic needs.\nThis scenario suggests a direct relationship between higher income and higher happiness levels across countries, which contradicts the Easterlin Paradox. The paradox states that beyond a certain point, increases in income do not lead to increases in happiness."}, {"n": 2, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "If set-point theory, the hedonic treadmill, and behavioural economics were all considered, then people who win the lottery and people with spinal cord injuries:", "options": ["experience temporary changes in happiness, but eventually, one is no less happy than the other, with losses felt more intensely than gains.", "do not experience significant changes in happiness because individuals are capable of adapting to the major events that happen in their lives.", "the presumption of their happiness changes, wherein people with spinal cord injuries will have a hard time reverting to the baseline of happiness.", "experience fluctuating happiness levels that continuously adapt to new circumstances without stabilising because gains and losses are perceived differently."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 45, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation\nIf set-point theory, the hedonic treadmill, and behavioural economics were all considered, then people who win the lottery and people with spinal cord injuries:\nThis question asks us to consider the implications of Set Point theory, the hedonic treadmill, and behavioural economics for people who experience significant life events, such as winning the lottery or suffering a spinal cord injury. We need to determine which option best captures the combined insights from these theories.\nTheory Summaries\n1. Set Point theory: Suggests individuals return to a baseline level of happiness after major life events.\n2. Hedonic treadmill: Proposes that people quickly return to a stable level of happiness despite major positive or negative changes.\n3. Behavioral economics: Highlights that individuals feel losses more intensely than gains.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(1) experience temporary changes in happiness, but eventually, one is no less happy than the other, with losses felt more intensely than gains.\nThis option best captures the combined insights from Set Point theory, the hedonic treadmill, and behavioural economics. It recognises that both lottery winners and those with spinal cord injuries will experience initial changes in happiness but will eventually return to their baseline levels. Additionally, it acknowledges that losses are felt more intensely than gains, consistent with behavioural economics.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(2) do not experience significant changes in happiness because individuals are capable of adapting to the major events that happen in their lives.\nThis option incorrectly suggests that individuals do not experience significant changes in happiness, ignoring the initial impact of major events. While adaptation is a key concept, the temporary changes are significant.\n(3) the presumption of their happiness changes, wherein people with spinal cord injuries will have a hard time reverting to the baseline of happiness.\nThis option conflicts with Set Point theory and the hedonic treadmill, which propose that individuals generally return to their baseline happiness levels over time, even after negative events.\n(4) experience fluctuating happiness levels that continuously adapt to new circumstances without stabilising because gains and losses are perceived differently.\nThis option implies that happiness levels do not stabilise, which contradicts the Set Point theory and the hedonic treadmill, both of which suggest a return to a baseline level of happiness."}, {"n": 3, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "Which of the following best sums up the author's interpretation of the policy impact of the Easterlin Paradox?", "options": ["Policies should not solely focus on economic growth, as wealthier countries and individuals within countries are generally happier, but increases in income over time do not lead to sustained increases in happiness.", "The Easterlin Paradox shows that while wealthier countries as a group are happier than poorer ones, within those wealthier countries, further income growth has little effect on average happiness levels.", "Even among the less happy, poorer countries, there is not a clear relationship between average income and happiness levels, suggesting that policy should focus on various cultural and non-economic factors.", "The paradox indicates that policies should consider factors beyond income, such as health, family, and job stability, as increases in income alone do not lead to sustained happiness."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 77, "solution": "Correct Answer – (4)\nQuestion Explanation\nWhich of the following best sums up the author's interpretation of the policy impact of the Easterlin Paradox?\nThis question asks us to determine the author's interpretation of the policy implications of the Easterlin Paradox. We need to understand the author's main argument regarding how policymakers should respond to the findings described by the Easterlin Paradox.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(4) The paradox indicates that policies should consider factors beyond income, such as health, family, and job stability, as increases in income alone do not lead to sustained happiness.\nThis option best captures the author's interpretation. The passage discusses how the Easterlin Paradox suggests that increases in income do not lead to sustained increases in happiness. Instead, other factors such as health, family, and job stability play a crucial role in well-being and should be considered in policymaking.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) Policies should not solely focus on economic growth, as wealthier countries and individuals within countries are generally happier, but increases in income over time do not lead to sustained increases in happiness.\nWhile this option touches on the idea that economic growth should not be the sole focus, it does not fully capture the author's emphasis on the importance of non-economic factors like health and family in achieving sustained happiness.\n(2) The Easterlin Paradox shows that while wealthier countries as a group are happier than poorer ones, within those wealthier countries, further income growth has little effect on average happiness levels.\nThis option describes a specific aspect of the Easterlin Paradox but does not address the broader policy implications the author is emphasising, particularly the need to focus on factors beyond income.\n(3) Even among the less happy, poorer countries, there is not a clear relationship between average income and happiness levels, suggesting that policy should focus on various cultural and non-economic factors.\nWhile this option highlights the lack of a clear relationship between income and happiness in poorer countries, it does not specifically address the author's interpretation of the policy impact, which includes the broader range of factors like health, family, and job stability."}, {"n": 4, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P1", "stem": "\"Easterlin argues that individuals adapt more in the pecuniary arena than in the non-pecuniary arena, while life-changing events, such as bereavement, have lasting effects on happiness.\" Which one of the following provides the most accurate interpretation of this statement?", "options": ["Like-changing events like grief can have a huge impact on happiness, even if one has been able to adapt to financial changes that have happened in the past.", "Individuals adapt more quickly to pecuniary changes than to major events in life, and these life-changing events have a more positive impact on their happiness.", "Individuals do not adjust as quickly to non-financial changes as they do to financial events, with some non-financial events significantly impacting happiness.", "Individuals cannot adjust to non-pecuniary changes, and even though they adapt to pecuniary changes, some life-changing events profoundly impact happiness."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 32, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation\n\"Easterlin argues that individuals adapt more in the pecuniary arena than in the non-pecuniary arena, while life-changing events, such as bereavement, have lasting effects on happiness.\" Which one of the following provides the most accurate interpretation of this statement?\nThis question requires us to interpret Easterlin's statement about the different adaptation rates to pecuniary and non-pecuniary changes and the lasting effects of life-changing events on happiness. We need to find the option that best reflects the comparative aspect of adaptation and the enduring impact of significant life events.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(3) Individuals do not adjust as quickly to non-financial changes as they do to financial events, with some non-financial events significantly impacting happiness.\nThis option accurately captures the essence of Easterlin's argument. It highlights two main points:\n1. Quicker Adaptation to Pecuniary Changes: Individuals tend to adapt relatively quickly to financial changes, such as increases in income. This means that while there might be a temporary boost in happiness from a raise or financial windfall, people generally return to their baseline happiness level over time.\n2. Slower Adaptation to Non-Pecuniary Changes: In contrast, individuals do not adjust as quickly to non-financial changes, especially significant life events. These events, such as the loss of a loved one (bereavement), have a profound and lasting impact on an individual's happiness.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) Life-changing events like grief can have a huge impact on happiness, even if one has been able to adapt to financial changes that have happened in the past.\nWhile this statement is true, it does not fully capture the comparative aspect of how quickly individuals adapt to pecuniary versus non-pecuniary changes. The focus should be on the relative speed of adaptation and the lasting impact of non-financial events.\n(2) Individuals adapt more quickly to pecuniary changes than to major events in life, and these life-changing events have a more positive impact on their happiness.\nThis option correctly states that individuals adapt more quickly to financial changes, but it incorrectly asserts that life-changing events have a more positive impact on happiness. Easterlin's argument is that these events have a lasting impact, which is not necessarily positive.\n(4) Individuals cannot adjust to non-pecuniary changes, and even though they adapt to pecuniary changes, some life-changing events profoundly impact happiness.\nThis option inaccurately states that individuals cannot adjust to non-pecuniary changes at all. Easterlin argues that individuals adapt more slowly to non-financial changes, not that they cannot adapt at all. Additionally, the statement fails to emphasise the relative speed of adaptation between pecuniary and non-pecuniary changes."}, {"n": 5, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "Which of the following is the primary conclusion of the study mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["Societal attitudes and values significantly influence people's reluctance to seek support from animal welfare charities despite their needs.", "There is resentment towards others perceived as taking advantage of the support system, leading to concerns about fairness and suggestions for improving the system.", "There are three dominant themes—‘giving back’, ‘sacrifice’, and ‘equity’—that illustrate how broader societal beliefs influence the identities and behaviours of individuals.", "Pet owners often prioritise their pets' needs above their own, demonstrating a deep commitment and willingness to make personal sacrifices."], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 26, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation\nWhich of the following is the primary conclusion of the study mentioned in the passage?\nThis question asks us to identify the primary conclusion of the study mentioned in the passage. The question requires us to understand the overall findings and key themes presented by the research. It is essential to focus on what the study primarily aims to convey about the influence of societal attitudes and values on people's behaviour regarding seeking support from animal welfare charities.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(1) Societal attitudes and values significantly influence people's reluctance to seek support from animal welfare charities despite their needs.\nThis option best captures the primary conclusion of the study. The passage details how broader societal beliefs, specifically those related to ‘giving back’, ‘sacrifice’, and ‘equity’, shape the identities and behaviours of individuals. These societal attitudes create a sense of moral obligation and expectations that influence whether people seek support from charities. The study highlights that: ‘Giving back’ reflects a cultural belief in reciprocating what one receives, leading people to feel obligated to donate or volunteer if they receive support. ‘Sacrifice’ demonstrates pet owners’ commitment to their pets, often prioritising their pets' needs above their own, which affects their decision to seek support. ‘Equity’ involves concerns about fairness in the support system, where individuals compare themselves to others and feel resentment towards those perceived as exploiting the system. These themes collectively show that societal values significantly impact individuals' reluctance to seek support, as they navigate these complex moral and social expectations.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(2) There is resentment towards others perceived as taking advantage of the support system, leading to concerns about fairness and suggestions for improving the system.\nWhile the passage does mention resentment and concerns about fairness, these points are part of the broader discussion on societal values rather than the primary conclusion. The resentment towards others and suggestions for improvement are specific findings that illustrate the theme of ‘equity’ but do not encompass the overall conclusion about the influence of societal attitudes.\n(3) There are three dominant themes—‘giving back’, ‘sacrifice’, and ‘equity’—that illustrate how broader societal beliefs influence the identities and behaviours of individuals.\nThis option accurately identifies the three themes discussed in the passage, but it does not fully capture the primary conclusion. The main point of the study is not just to identify these themes but to show how they lead to a reluctance to seek support. The emphasis is on the influence of societal attitudes on behaviour, particularly in the context of seeking help from animal welfare charities.\n(4) Pet owners often prioritise their pets' needs above their own, demonstrating a deep commitment and willingness to make personal sacrifices.\nAlthough this finding is significant and well-supported in the passage, it is not the primary conclusion of the study. This point specifically relates to the theme of ‘sacrifice’ but does not address the broader conclusion about societal attitudes and values influencing people's behaviour regarding seeking support."}, {"n": 6, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "Based on the passage, all of the following are reasons why participants might feel resentment towards others receiving support from Blue Cross EXCEPT:", "options": ["others appear to be taking advantage of the system.", "others do not make any donations at all.", "others are not making any sacrifices on their part.", "others receive more support than is necessary."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 76, "solution": "Correct Answer – (4)\nQuestion Explanation\nBased on the passage, all of the following are reasons why participants might feel resentment towards others receiving support from Blue Cross EXCEPT:\nThis question requires us to identify the reason that is not mentioned in the passage as a source of participants' resentment towards others receiving support from Blue Cross. To answer this, we need to review the reasons provided in the passage and identify the one that is excluded.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(4) Others receive more support than is necessary.\nThis option is not mentioned in the passage as a reason for resentment. The passage discusses resentment towards those who appear to be taking advantage of the system, not making donations, or not making sacrifices, but it does not mention resentment based on the perception that others receive more support than necessary.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) Others appear to be taking advantage of the system.\nThe passage clearly states that participants feel resentment towards those who seem to be exploiting the support system. This is linked to concerns about fairness and the idea that some people are \"taking advantage\" without contributing back.\n(2) Others do not make any donations at all.\nThe passage mentions that there is resentment towards individuals who do not make any donations while receiving support. Participants compare themselves to these individuals and feel that it is unfair for some to receive support without giving anything in return.\n(3) Others are not making any sacrifices on their part.\nThe passage discusses how participants feel that some individuals do not prioritise their pets or make sacrifices, contributing to the sense of unfairness and resentment. Participants express that they themselves make sacrifices, and they expect others to do the same."}, {"n": 7, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "What could be the most likely reason for the author to mention 'a good moral citizen'?", "options": ["to emphasise the values that influence pet owners' decisions to seek or not seek support", "to argue that those who do not meet certain moral standards should be excluded from receiving support", "to illustrate the tension between personal sacrifice and the perceived fairness of the support system", "to illustrate how moral values shape behaviours and attitudes towards pet ownership"], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 29, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1)\nQuestion Explanation\nWhat could be the most likely reason for the author to mention 'a good moral citizen'?\nThis question asks us to infer the author's reason for mentioning 'a good moral citizen' in the passage. We need to understand how this concept fits within the overall discussion about the behaviours and attitudes of pet owners in seeking support from animal welfare charities.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(1) To emphasise the values that influence pet owners' decisions to seek or not seek support\nThis option accurately reflects the author's purpose. The passage discusses how societal values, such as 'giving back', 'sacrifice', and 'equity', influence pet owners' decisions regarding seeking support from charities. The concept of 'a good moral citizen' encapsulates these values, showing how they impact the willingness of individuals to ask for help.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(2) To argue that those who do not meet certain moral standards should be excluded from receiving support\nThe passage does not advocate for excluding individuals based on moral standards. Instead, it highlights the perceptions and attitudes of pet owners towards those who are perceived as exploiting the system, but it does not suggest that exclusion is the solution.\n(3) To illustrate the tension between personal sacrifice and the perceived fairness of the support system\nWhile the passage does touch upon personal sacrifice and fairness, this is not the main reason for mentioning 'a good moral citizen'. The primary purpose is to highlight the values that drive decisions about seeking support, rather than focusing on the tension itself.\n(4) To illustrate how moral values shape behaviours and attitudes towards pet ownership\nThis option is less accurate because the passage does not focus specifically on pet ownership. Instead, it addresses how moral values influence decisions related to seeking support from animal welfare charities. The concept of 'a good moral citizen' is used to highlight these broader values and their impact on behaviour."}, {"n": 8, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P2", "stem": "It can be inferred from the passage that each of the following contributes to why pet owners might not seek support EXCEPT:", "options": ["they prioritise their pets' needs over their own, often preferring personal sacrifices to requesting external assistance.", "they feel a moral obligation to reciprocate support, leading to hesitation in seeking help if they can't adequately give back.", "they feel that seeking support would lead to a perception that they are not a good moral citizen who prioritises the welfare of their pets.", "concerns about fairness and others taking advantage of the system make some pet owners reluctant to seek support."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 74, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation\nIt can be inferred from the passage that each of the following contributes to why pet owners might not seek support EXCEPT:\nThis question asks us to identify which statement is not a reason why pet owners might refrain from seeking support, based on the passage. We need to review the passage for the specific reasons mentioned and identify the one that is not listed as a contributing factor.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(3) They feel that seeking support would lead to a perception that they are not a good moral citizen who prioritises the welfare of their pets.\nThis option is not supported by the passage. The passage does not suggest that pet owners avoid seeking support because they fear being perceived as not prioritising the welfare of their pets. In fact, the passage highlights that pet owners often prioritise their pets' needs and make personal sacrifices, which is viewed positively within the moral framework discussed.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) They prioritise their pets' needs over their own, often preferring personal sacrifices to requesting external assistance.\nThe passage clearly indicates that pet owners often make personal sacrifices to prioritise their pets' needs, which can lead them to avoid seeking external assistance. This aligns with the theme of 'sacrifice' mentioned in the study.\n(2) They feel a moral obligation to reciprocate support, leading to hesitation in seeking help if they can't adequately give back.\nThe passage discusses the cultural belief in 'giving back', where individuals feel a moral obligation to reciprocate any support they receive. This can cause hesitation in seeking help if they feel unable to reciprocate adequately, as noted in the participants' narratives.\n(4) Concerns about fairness and others taking advantage of the system make some pet owners reluctant to seek support.\nThe passage mentions that some pet owners feel resentment towards those who appear to be exploiting the support system. This concern about fairness can make them reluctant to seek support themselves, as they do not want to be perceived as taking advantage."}, {"n": 9, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "The author mentions the avalanches in a sand-pile primarily in order to:", "options": ["compare it with the working of our brains.", "argue that disorder is necessary to create order.", "explain the mechanism of symmetry in neuroscience.", "highlight the unpredictable nature of physical phenomena."], "correct": "B", "cl_accuracy": 79, "solution": "Correct Answer – (2)\nQuestion Explanation\nThe author mentions the avalanches in a sand-pile primarily in order to: This question asks us to identify the primary reason the author mentions avalanches in a sand-pile. We need to understand how this example fits into the broader argument about symmetry, particularly the relationship between order and disorder.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(2) argue that disorder is necessary to create order.\nThe mention of avalanches in a sand-pile serves to illustrate how disorder (the avalanches) is used to maintain order within the sand-pile. This example supports the broader argument that there is a fundamental symmetry between order and disorder, and that disorder can be harnessed to create and maintain order. This demonstrates the interdependence between order and disorder as a key aspect of symmetry.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) compare it with the working of our brains.\nWhile the sand-pile model is compared to the brain's functioning, this is not the primary reason for mentioning the avalanches. The focus is on the broader principle of symmetry between order and disorder.\n(3) explain the mechanism of symmetry in neuroscience.\nAlthough the sand-pile model is used to draw parallels with neuroscience, the primary purpose of mentioning the avalanches is to illustrate the fundamental interdependence between order and disorder, not to explain the specific mechanism of symmetry in neuroscience.\n(4) highlight the unpredictable nature of physical phenomena.\nThe avalanches in the sand-pile are used to illustrate the concept of disorder being harnessed to maintain order, rather than to emphasise the unpredictability of physical phenomena. The primary focus is on the relationship between order and disorder."}, {"n": 10, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "Which one of the following best sums up the key idea of the last paragraph?", "options": ["Nature, as a designer, achieves balance and functionality through straightforward and elegant symmetrical principles.", "Universe inherently contains both symmetrical and asymmetrical elements, leading to a cacophony of orderly and chaotic aspects.", "Symmetry inherently conditions itself with infinite reach, uniting order and disorder to structure the cosmos.", "The organisation of order and chaos is a key principle underlying many aspects of our understanding of the universe."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 81, "solution": "(Note: Passage Explanations for Q9 to 12 — same 6-paragraph passage explanation as Q9; not repeated verbatim here but emitted with Q10 per duplication rule.)\n\nCorrect Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation:\nWhich one of the following best sums up the key idea of the last paragraph?\nThe question asks for the best summary of the key idea of the last paragraph. The paragraph discusses how symmetry is a fundamental principle that conditions itself, leading to a universe structured by the interplay between order and disorder. It emphasises that symmetry has infinite reach, encompassing everything, including itself, and explains how this results in a universe that inherently includes both order and chaos.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\n(3) Symmetry inherently conditions itself with infinite reach, uniting order and disorder to structure the cosmos.\nThis option effectively captures the central idea of the last paragraph. The paragraph states that symmetry is subject only to itself and has infinite reach, implying that it extends to all aspects of the universe. It unites order and disorder, suggesting that these seemingly opposing forces are interconnected through the principle of symmetry. The phrase \"structure the cosmos\" accurately reflects the idea that the universe's organisation is based on this fundamental symmetry.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\n(1) Nature, as a designer, achieves balance and functionality through straightforward and elegant symmetrical principles.\nThis option misinterprets the main point of the paragraph. While the paragraph does mention that the best designs are simple and that Nature is an effective designer, the primary focus is on the self-conditioning and infinite reach of symmetry. This option oversimplifies the idea by focusing on balance and functionality rather than the deeper concept of symmetry uniting order and disorder.\n(2) Universe inherently contains both symmetrical and asymmetrical elements, leading to a cacophony of orderly and chaotic aspects.\nThis option introduces the idea of asymmetrical elements and describes the universe as having a \"cacophony\" of orderly and chaotic aspects, which is not accurate. The paragraph focuses on the symmetry between order and disorder, not asymmetry. Additionally, \"cacophony\" implies a sense of discord or noise, which does not align with the idea of symmetry, providing a structured and interconnected explanation for the universe.\n(4) The organisation of order and chaos is a key principle underlying many aspects of our understanding of the universe.\nThis option is too vague and general. While it is true that the organisation of order and chaos is discussed, the key idea of the paragraph is more specific: symmetry inherently conditions itself, has infinite reach, and unites order and disorder. This option fails to capture the self-referential nature of symmetry and its comprehensive scope, as described in the paragraph."}, {"n": 11, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "Which one of the following is the most accurate definition of symmetry, based on the passage?", "options": ["Symmetry is the fundamental principle that explains the paradoxical coexistence of order and chaos in the universe.", "Symmetry is the repeating occurrence of identical patterns, creating harmony in natural laws and designs.", "Symmetry is the principle that encompasses the predictable patterns seen in both physical and social systems.", "Symmetry is the concept that reveals the balance and interdependence between order and disorder in various phenomena."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 62, "solution": "(Passage Explanations for Q9 to 12 same passage explanation block; emitted with Q11 per duplication rule.)\n\nCorrect Answer – (4)\nQuestion Explanation:\nThe question asks for the most accurate definition of symmetry based on the passage. The passage discusses symmetry as a principle that reveals the relationship between order and disorder, suggesting that symmetry is a fundamental aspect of the universe's structure and manifests in various phenomena.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\n(4) Symmetry is the concept that reveals the balance and interdependence between order and disorder in various phenomena.\nThis option accurately reflects the passage's discussion of symmetry. The passage highlights how symmetry reveals the fundamental interdependence between order and disorder, as seen in examples like the brain's functioning, crowd behaviour, and other natural and social phenomena. This definition encapsulates the idea that symmetry is not just about identical patterns but about the balance and interdependence of seemingly opposite forces.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\n(1) Symmetry is the fundamental principle that explains the paradoxical coexistence of order and chaos in the universe.\nWhile this option captures part of the passage's discussion, it emphasises the paradoxical coexistence of order and chaos rather than the balance and interdependence between them. The passage focuses more on the interdependence and how symmetry unites these elements, making this option slightly less accurate.\n(2) Symmetry is the repeating occurrence of identical patterns, creating harmony in natural laws and designs.\nThis option simplifies symmetry to just the repetition of identical patterns, which does not fully capture the passage's deeper discussion of symmetry. The passage goes beyond just patterns and talks about the interdependence of order and disorder, making this option too narrow.\n(3) Symmetry is the principle that encompasses the predictable patterns seen in both physical and social systems.\nWhile the passage does mention predictable patterns in physical and social systems, it emphasises the broader concept of symmetry as the balance and interdependence between order and disorder. This option is somewhat accurate but does not fully capture the comprehensive nature of symmetry as described in the passage."}, {"n": 12, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P3", "stem": "Each of the following is a valid inference from the passage EXCEPT:", "options": ["behaviours that appear chaotic can actually conform to predictable patterns.", "the idea that disorder can help maintain order is an observable phenomenon.", "the balance that exists between order and disorder in symmetry is paradoxical.", "a well-functioning order system can experience disorder as a natural process."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 36, "solution": "(Passage Explanations for Q9 to 12 same passage explanation block; emitted with Q12 per duplication rule.)\n\nCorrect Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation:\nEach of the following is a valid inference from the passage EXCEPT:\nThe question asks to identify the statement that is NOT a valid inference from the passage. The passage discusses various examples and explanations of symmetry, particularly focusing on the interplay between order and disorder, and how what may seem chaotic can actually conform to predictable patterns.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\n(3) the balance that exists between order and disorder in symmetry is paradoxical.\nWhile the passage mentions that it may seem paradoxical to suggest that nature's ordering principle encompasses a symmetry between order and disorder, it does not explicitly state that this balance is paradoxical. Instead, it explains that symmetry inherently unites order and disorder, suggesting that this relationship is fundamental rather than paradoxical.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\n(1) behaviours that appear chaotic can actually conform to predictable patterns.\nThis is a valid inference from the passage. The passage discusses how crowd behaviour, which may seem chaotic to a layperson, actually conforms to predictable patterns determined by factors like geometric boundary conditions or time gap distributions.\n(2) the idea that disorder can help maintain order is an observable phenomenon.\nThis is also a valid inference. The passage gives the example of the sand-pile model, where random and unpredictable avalanches (disorder) help maintain the overall order of the sand-pile.\n(4) a well-functioning order system can experience disorder as a natural process.\nThis is a valid inference from the passage. The passage discusses how order and disorder are interdependent, implying that a well-functioning system can naturally experience disorder as part of maintaining overall order, as seen in examples like the brain's functioning and traffic flows."}, {"n": 13, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "The 'existential crisis' mentioned in the passage most likely refers to the belief that British colonials' withdrawal from India:", "options": ["would prevent their maintenance of a self-sustaining military stronghold.", "would jeopardise British economic interests and investments.", "would undermine British imperial authority and self-identity.", "would erode the perceived operational efficiency of British governance."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 40, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation\nThe 'existential crisis' mentioned in the passage most likely refers to the belief that British colonials' withdrawal from India:\nThis question asks us to determine what the author means by the \"existential crisis\" mentioned in the passage. We need to understand the context in which the term is used and infer the belief or concern that withdrawal from India would trigger among British colonials.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(3) would undermine British imperial authority and self-identity.\nThe passage describes how the entrenched belief in Indian dominion became a core ideology among imperial families, and any suggestion of withdrawal was seen as an existential crisis. This implies that withdrawal would fundamentally challenge their sense of authority and identity as imperial rulers. The existential crisis refers to the deep fear that losing India would mean losing a significant part of their imperial identity and authority.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) would prevent their maintenance of a self-sustaining military stronghold.\nWhile the passage does mention that India was transformed into a vital military stronghold for Britain, the existential crisis refers to something more profound than just military logistics. It is about the fundamental challenge to their authority and identity.\n(2) would jeopardise British economic interests and investments.\nAlthough economic interests and investments are important, the passage emphasises a deeper concern related to authority and identity. The existential crisis is more about the ideological and psychological implications of losing their colonial hold, rather than just economic losses.\n(4) would erode the perceived operational efficiency of British governance.\nThe passage critiques the myth of operational efficiency, suggesting it was never a reality. The existential crisis is about the loss of authority and identity, not just the perception of governance efficiency."}, {"n": 14, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "The critics of the British colonial rule seemingly believe that the British colonialism:", "options": ["advanced commercial interests rather than societal upliftment.", "while operationally efficient, was morally untenable.", "was operationally inefficient, rooted in underlying turmoil.", "led to the militarisation of commerce and strategic fortifications."], "correct": "B", "cl_accuracy": 43, "solution": "Correct Answer – (2)\nQuestion Explanation\nThe critics of the British colonial rule seemingly believe that the British colonialism:\nThis question requires us to determine what critics of British colonial rule believe about British colonialism according to the passage. Specifically, we need to focus on the criticisms highlighted by the passage and how these criticisms are framed in terms of operational efficiency and moral evaluations.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(2) while operationally efficient, was morally untenable.\nThe passage indicates that both supporters and critics of British rule in India generally agree on its perceived operational efficiency but differ in their moral evaluations. The key sentence states: \"Advocates and critics alike generally agree on the perceived operational efficiency of British governance, diverging primarily over ethical assessments.\" This suggests that critics do not dispute the effectiveness or efficiency of British governance but criticise it on moral grounds. The critics most likely believe that despite the apparent order and systematic administration, British colonialism was morally wrong due to its underlying turmoil, violence, and exploitation.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) advanced commercial interests rather than societal upliftment.\nWhile it is true that British colonialism advanced commercial interests over societal upliftment, this is not the central argument presented by the critics in the context of the passage. The focus of the critics, as highlighted, is on the moral untenability rather than just the prioritisation of commercial interests.\n(3) was operationally inefficient, rooted in underlying turmoil.\nThis option contradicts the passage's statement that both advocates and critics agree on the perceived operational efficiency of British governance. The critics do not argue that the governance was inefficient but that it was morally wrong despite its efficiency.\n(4) led to the militarisation of commerce and strategic fortifications.\nAlthough the militarisation of commerce and strategic fortifications are mentioned in the passage, this point does not address the critics' main argument regarding the moral evaluations of British governance. The focus is on the broader moral implications rather than just specific actions like militarisation."}, {"n": 15, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "The author mentions each of the following as the source of motivation for the British Raj EXCEPT:", "options": ["deep-seated fear of losing control", "pursuit of military prestige", "ambition to grow the British Empire", "apprehensions concerning rivals"], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 41, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nQuestion Explanation\nThe author mentions each of the following as the source of motivation for the British Raj EXCEPT:\nThis question asks us to identify which of the given motivations is not mentioned in the passage as a source of motivation for the British Raj. We need to carefully review the passage to understand the motivations described by the author and determine which one is excluded.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(3) ambition to grow the British Empire\nThis option is not explicitly mentioned in the passage as a source of motivation for the British Raj. The passage discusses various motivations, such as fear of losing control, pursuit of military prestige, and apprehensions concerning rivals, but it does not specifically mention an ambition to grow the British Empire as a driving factor.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) deep-seated fear of losing control\nThe passage indicates that the British were motivated by a deep-seated fear of losing control. It mentions \"profound anxiety\" and \"paranoid defence,\" reflecting the fear that drove their actions in India. This fear is evident in the British efforts to maintain their power and respond to crises, such as the construction of fortifications and the militarisation of commerce.\n(2) pursuit of military prestige\nThe passage explicitly mentions the \"allure of military glory\" as a motivation for the British Raj. The desire for military prestige is highlighted as a factor driving the British to establish fortified outposts and engage in conquest. This pursuit of glory contributed to the expansion of British control in India.\n(4) apprehensions concerning rivals\nThe passage discusses the British apprehensions concerning rivals, particularly the competition from European merchants and Indian rulers. It mentions \"anxieties over competition,\" which led to the militarisation of trade and the establishment of strategic fortifications. These apprehensions played a significant role in shaping British actions in India."}, {"n": 16, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": "P4", "stem": "The author would agree with each of the following statements EXCEPT:", "options": ["the rapid British withdrawal in 1947 is a tacit admission of the unsustainable nature of their control and governance.", "the British Raj's administrative decisions were often improvisational, revealing an underlying lack of coherent governance strategy.", "the British Raj's efforts to control India frequently fueled a cycle of repressive measures and subsequent resistance.", "the socio-economic landscape of India profoundly altered British policies, leading to structural poverty and instability."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 50, "solution": "Correct Answer – (4)\nQuestion Explanation\nThe author would agree with each of the following statements EXCEPT:\nThis question asks us to determine which statement the author would not agree with based on the passage. We need to carefully review the passage to understand the author's arguments and conclusions about the British Raj and its impact on India.\nCorrect Answer Explanation\n(4) the socio-economic landscape of India profoundly altered British policies, leading to structural poverty and instability.\nThis statement suggests that changes in India's socio-economic landscape drove British policy changes. However, the passage primarily argues that British policies were reactionary and driven by their own crises and security concerns rather than being profoundly altered by India's socio-economic conditions. While the result of British policies was indeed structural poverty and instability, the driving force behind these policies was not the socio-economic landscape of India itself.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations\n(1) the rapid British withdrawal in 1947 is a tacit admission of the unsustainable nature of their control and governance.\nThe author would likely agree with this statement. The passage discusses how, by 1946, even Britons doubted their state's capacity to ensure safety, and the rapid withdrawal in 1947 underscored the erosion of confidence in their governance, indicating it was unsustainable.\n(2) the British Raj's administrative decisions were often improvisational, revealing an underlying lack of coherent governance strategy.\nThe author would agree with this statement. The passage describes British infrastructure projects and policies as frantic responses to crises rather than as part of a deliberate, coherent strategy. This improvisational approach is highlighted as a significant flaw in their governance.\n(3) the British Raj's efforts to control India frequently fueled a cycle of repressive measures and subsequent resistance.\nThe author would agree with this statement. The passage details how British security paranoia and repressive measures, such as the massacre at Jallianwala Bagh, fueled cycles of violence and resistance among the Indian population, illustrating this dynamic."}, {"n": 17, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "There is a sentence that is missing in the paragraph below. Look at the paragraph and decide where (option 1, 2, 3, or 4) the following sentence would best fit.\nSentence: The maps don't see the world the way the people who use them do.\nParagraph: Mapping software is incredible. __1__. Having instant access to every storefront, building, park, and transit stop on every street, almost anywhere in the world, has changed my life as much as any other single innovation of the cellphone age. __2__. But also, mapping software is a little weird. Seemingly random places show up as landmarks in my neighborhood: a Bitcoin ATM, a nearby hotel I'll never stay at. __3__. And when I need directions, my app likes to tell me things that no one ever needs to know, such as when to merge onto the freeway from an on-ramp. Why is it so obtuse? Or perhaps the better question is: What makes the software think that I'm obtuse? __4__.", "options": ["Option 2", "Option 1", "Option 4", "Option 3"], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 67, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3) Option 4\nAnalysis of the Missing Sentence:\n\"The maps don't see the world the way the people who use them do.\"\nClues: This sentence indicates a contrast between how maps/software represent the world and how people perceive it. It suggests a misalignment or difference in perspective.\nEvaluation of Each Blank:\nOption 1:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"Mapping software is incredible. 1. Having instant access to every storefront, building, park, and transit stop on every street, almost anywhere in the world, has changed my life as much as any other single innovation of the cellphone age.\"\n• Context: The sentences discuss the positive aspects of mapping software and its impact.\n• Analysis: Inserting the missing sentence here would disrupt the flow of praising the software, as it introduces a contrasting idea.\n• Fit: Poor fit.\nOption 2:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"Having instant access to every storefront, building, park, and transit stop on every street, almost anywhere in the world, has changed my life as much as any other single innovation of the cellphone age. 2. But also, mapping software is a little weird.\"\n• Context: The first sentence concludes the benefits of the mapping software, and the sentence following the blank starts introducing some oddities.\n• Analysis: Placing the missing sentence here would interfere with the contrast signaled by \"But,\" which marks the transition to discussing the peculiarities.\n• Fit: Poor fit.\nOption 3:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"Seemingly random places show up as landmarks in my neighborhood: a Bitcoin ATM, a nearby hotel I'll never stay at. 3. And when I need directions, my app likes to tell me things that no one ever needs to know, such as when to merge onto the freeway from an on-ramp.\"\n• Context: This part of the paragraph gives specific examples of the oddities in mapping software.\n• Analysis: The missing sentence does not fit well here as it does not align with the specific examples being given.\n• Fit: Poor fit.\nOption 4:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"Why is it so obtuse? Or perhaps the better question is: What makes the software think that I'm obtuse? 4.\"\n• Context: This part of the paragraph questions the software's perspective and usability.\n• Analysis: The missing sentence fits well here as it aligns with the questioning of the software's perspective and provides a lead into the concluding question.\n• Fit: Excellent fit.\nConclusion:\nThe best place for the sentence \"The maps don't see the world the way the people who use them do.\" is Option 4. This placement allows for a smooth transition from discussing the peculiarities and the potential misunderstandings caused by the software to the concluding question about the software's perspective.\nRevised Paragraph:\nMapping software is incredible. Having instant access to every storefront, building, park, and transit stop on every street, almost anywhere in the world, has changed my life as much as any other single innovation of the cellphone age. But also, mapping software is a little weird. Seemingly random places show up as landmarks in my neighborhood: a Bitcoin ATM, a nearby hotel I'll never stay at. And when I need directions, my app likes to tell me things that no one ever needs to know, such as when to merge onto the freeway from an on-ramp. Why is it so obtuse? Or perhaps the better question is: What makes the software think that I'm obtuse? The maps don't see the world the way the people who use them do."}, {"n": 18, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "There is a sentence that is missing in the paragraph below. Look at the paragraph and decide where (option 1, 2, 3, or 4) the following sentence would best fit.\nSentence: What is reported to, and handled by, the protectorate offices in sending countries is only the tip of the iceberg.\nParagraph: The Gulf migrant needs protection in the sending as well as receiving country. __1__. When abuse occurs, recruitment agents and employers are the two major violators of the migrant's rights. Recruitment agents who arrange the employment for a majority of workers sometimes charge up to ten or fifteen times more than they are allowed officially. __2__. In some cases, the workers are abandoned in the host country or a third country of transit. __3__. Rules and procedures exist for punishing fraudulent agents. Yet, many loopholes remain. __4__.", "options": ["Option 4", "Option 1", "Option 3", "Option 2"], "correct": "A", "cl_accuracy": 41, "solution": "Correct Answer – (1) Option 4\nAnalysis of the Missing Sentence:\n\"What is reported to, and handled by, the protectorate offices in sending countries is only the tip of the iceberg.\"\nClues: This sentence indicates that the issues reported to protectorate offices are minimal compared to the actual problems faced by migrants. It suggests a larger, unreported set of issues.\nEvaluation of Each Blank:\nOption 1:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"The Gulf migrant needs protection in the sending as well as receiving country. 1. When abuse occurs, recruitment agents and employers are the two major violators of the migrant's rights.\"\n• Context: The first sentence introduces the need for protection in both countries, and the following sentence identifies key violators of migrants' rights.\n• Analysis: The missing sentence does not fit well here as it disrupts the logical flow from the need for protection to identifying violators.\n• Fit: Poor fit.\nOption 2:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"When abuse occurs, recruitment agents and employers are the two major violators of the migrant's rights. 2. In some cases, the workers are abandoned in the host country or a third country of transit.\"\n• Context: The sentences discuss the abuses by recruitment agents and the subsequent abandonment of workers.\n• Analysis: The missing sentence does not fit well here as it interrupts the flow of discussing the specific abuses and consequences.\n• Fit: Poor fit.\nOption 3:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"Recruitment agents who arrange the employment for a majority of workers sometimes charge up to ten or fifteen times more than they are allowed officially. 3. Rules and procedures exist for punishing fraudulent agents.\"\n• Context: The sentences discuss the overcharging by recruitment agents and the existence of rules to punish fraudulent agents.\n• Analysis: The missing sentence does not fit well here as it does not directly relate to the discussion of overcharging and existing rules.\n• Fit: Poor fit.\nOption 4:\n• Sentences before and after the blank: \"Rules and procedures exist for punishing fraudulent agents. 4. Yet, many loopholes remain.\"\n• Context: The sentences discuss the existence of rules and the remaining loopholes.\n• Analysis: The missing sentence fits well here as it underscores that the issues handled by protectorate offices are minimal compared to the larger problem, leading into the statement about loopholes.\n• Fit: Excellent fit.\nConclusion:\nThe best place for the sentence \"What is reported to, and handled by, the protectorate offices in sending countries is only the tip of the iceberg.\" is Option 4. This placement allows for a smooth transition from the existence of rules to the acknowledgment of remaining loopholes, emphasizing the larger, unaddressed issues.\nRevised Paragraph:\nThe Gulf migrant needs protection in the sending as well as receiving country. When abuse occurs, recruitment agents and employers are the two major violators of the migrant's rights. Recruitment agents who arrange the employment for a majority of workers sometimes charge up to ten or fifteen times more than they are allowed officially. In some cases, the workers are abandoned in the host country or a third country of transit. Rules and procedures exist for punishing fraudulent agents. Yet, many loopholes remain.\nWhat is reported to, and handled by, the protectorate offices in sending countries is only the tip of the iceberg."}, {"n": 19, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "Five jumbled up sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5), related to a topic, are given below. Four of them can be put together to form a coherent paragraph. Identify the odd sentence and key in the number of that sentence as your answer.\n1. Two such models, drug self-administration and intracranial self-stimulation, have proven useful in addressing the influence of pain on both pharmacology and behavior.\n2. While historically preclinical pain studies have relied heavily on reflexive withdrawal behaviors as behavioral endpoints, the drug abuse research community by necessity has developed a number of validated models to access subjective states in laboratory animals.\n3. Pain is appreciated to be primarily a subjective state in humans, rather than solely a physiological condition consisting primarily of hypersensitivity to various peripheral stimuli.\n4. The models have been used extensively to address neurobiological mechanisms of genetics, environment, and drugs on complex behavior.\n5. They should prove useful in the future for developing novel pain therapies that have less abuse potential than those currently employed.", "options": [], "correct": "3", "cl_accuracy": 56, "solution": "Correct Answer – 3\nAnalysis of Each Sentence:\nSentence 1: \"Two such models, drug self-administration and intracranial self-stimulation, have proven useful in addressing the influence of pain on both pharmacology and behavior.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Introduces two models that are useful in pain and behavior research.\nClues:\n• \"Two such models\": Refers back to models previously mentioned, indicating continuation from earlier discussion.\n• Focuses specifically on two models and their applications.\nSentence 2: \"While historically preclinical pain studies have relied heavily on reflexive withdrawal behaviors as behavioral endpoints, the drug abuse research community by necessity has developed a number of validated models to access subjective states in laboratory animals.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Contrasts traditional pain study methods with newer models developed by the drug abuse research community.\nClues:\n• \"While historically\": Indicates a contrast between past methods and current advancements.\n• Sets the stage by providing background information and context for the development of new models.\nSentence 3: \"Pain is appreciated to be primarily a subjective state in humans, rather than solely a physiological condition consisting primarily of hypersensitivity to various peripheral stimuli.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Defines pain as a subjective state in humans.\nClues:\n• Provides a general definition of pain, not specific to any models or research methods discussed.\nSentence 4: \"The models have been used extensively to address neurobiological mechanisms of genetics, environment, and drugs on complex behavior.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Describes the extensive use of the models in addressing neurobiological mechanisms.\nClues:\n\"The models\": Refers back to previously mentioned models, indicating continuity in the discussion.\n• Focuses specifically on the applications of the models in research.\nSentence 5: \"They should prove useful in the future for developing novel pain therapies that have less abuse potential than those currently employed.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Predicts the future usefulness of the models in developing pain therapies.\nClues:\n• \"They should\": Refers back to the models, indicating a forward-looking statement about their potential.\n• Focuses on a specific prediction about the models' future use.\nLogical Sequencing:\n• Sentence 2 should start the paragraph by setting up the historical context and development of new models.\n• Sentence 4 follows by describing the extensive use of these models.\n• Sentence 1 specifies the two models and their use in pain research.\n• Sentence 5 concludes with a prediction about the future use of these models.\nTherefore, the sequence is: 2, 4, 1, 5.\nCoherent Paragraph:\n\"While historically preclinical pain studies have relied heavily on reflexive withdrawal behaviors as behavioral endpoints, the drug abuse research community by necessity has developed a number of validated models to access subjective states in laboratory animals. The models have been used extensively to address neurobiological mechanisms of genetics, environment, and drugs on complex behavior. Two such models, drug self-administration and intracranial self-stimulation, have proven useful in addressing the influence of pain on both pharmacology and behavior. They should prove useful in the future for developing novel pain therapies that have less abuse potential than those currently employed.\"\nOdd Sentence:\nSentence 3: This sentence, which defines pain as a subjective state in humans, does not fit with the rest of the paragraph that focuses on specific models used in preclinical pain and drug research."}, {"n": 20, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "Five jumbled up sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5), related to a topic, are given below. Four of them can be put together to form a coherent paragraph. Identify the odd sentence and key in the number of that sentence as your answer.\n1. For the effects to appear, long-term follow-up is not necessary, as the repercussions of unemployment can occur soon after unemployment has started and often before if the redundancy is preceded by a period of insecurity.\n2. Unemployment plays the role of the disastrous event, in response to which the indigenous workers may do something constructive to eliminate problems, or regulate the emotions arising from unemployment.\n3. The most often studied outcomes are various self-reported measures of common mental disorders.\n4. Best evidence comes from factory closure studies and longitudinal studies that have followed participants into unemployment and/or reemployment.\n5. The effects of unemployment on different domains of mental health are by now well established.", "options": [], "correct": "2", "cl_accuracy": 25, "solution": "Correct Answer – 2\nAnalysis of Each Sentence:\nSentence 1: \"For the effects to appear, long-term follow-up is not necessary, as the repercussions of unemployment can occur soon after unemployment has started and often before if the redundancy is preceded by a period of insecurity.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Discusses the timing of the effects of unemployment.\nClues:\n• \"For the effects to appear\": Focuses on the immediacy of the effects of unemployment.\n• Specific discussion on the timing of unemployment effects.\nSentence 2: \"Unemployment plays the role of the disastrous event, in response to which the indigenous workers may do something constructive to eliminate problems, or regulate the emotions arising from unemployment.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Discusses the response of workers to unemployment.\nClues:\n• \"Plays the role\": Unemployment as a triggering event for actions by workers.\n• General statement about reactions to unemployment.\nSentence 3: \"The most often studied outcomes are various self-reported measures of common mental disorders.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Mentions the common mental health outcomes studied in relation to unemployment.\nClues:\n• \"Most often studied outcomes\": Focuses on the type of outcomes studied.\n• Specific focus on mental health outcomes.\nSentence 4: \"Best evidence comes from factory closure studies and longitudinal studies that have followed participants into unemployment and/or reemployment.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Provides sources of evidence for studying the effects of unemployment.\nClues:\n• \"Best evidence\": Refers to studies providing strong evidence.\n• Specific sources of evidence.\nSentence 5: \"The effects of unemployment on different domains of mental health are by now well established.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: States that the effects of unemployment on mental health are well known.\nClues:\n• \"By now well established\" : Asserts the established knowledge of unemployment effects.\n• General statement about the established effects.\nLogical Sequencing:\n• Sentence 5 should start the paragraph by stating that the effects of unemployment on mental health are well established.\n• Sentence 3 follows by mentioning the common mental health outcomes studied.\n• Sentence 1 discusses the timing of these effects, elaborating on how soon they can occur.\n• Sentence 4 provides the evidence supporting these observations.\nTherefore, the sequence is: 5, 3, 1, 4.\nCoherent Paragraph:\n\"The effects of unemployment on different domains of mental health are by now well established. The most often studied outcomes are various self-reported measures of common mental disorders. For the effects to appear, long-term follow-up is not necessary, as the repercussions of unemployment can occur soon after unemployment has started and often before if the redundancy is preceded by a period of insecurity. Best evidence comes from factory closure studies and longitudinal studies that have followed participants into unemployment and/or reemployment.\"\nOdd Sentence:\nSentence 2: This sentence, which discusses the role of unemployment as a disastrous event and workers' responses, does not fit with the rest of the paragraph that focuses on the mental health effects and evidence from studies."}, {"n": 21, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The four sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3 and 4) given below, when properly sequenced, would yield a coherent paragraph. Decide on the proper sequencing of the order of the sentences and key in the sequence of the four numbers as your answer.\n1. But he must have been a charismatic figure, chosen by a group of pirates as their leader over another captain with long experience of raiding in the Indian Ocean.\n2. He was last seen setting out alone, after landing with his remaining shipmates on the north-east coast of Ireland.\n3. Henry Every remains a phantom—his birth, possibly in Devon, remains obscure, and we don't know when or how he died.\n4. Since he never boarded the Gunsway himself, he is presented as an almost gentlemanly figure — above the fray, but powerless to stop the violence he had unleashed.", "options": [], "correct": "3214", "cl_accuracy": 29, "solution": "Correct Answer – 3214\nAnalysis of Each Sentence:\nSentence 1: \"But he must have been a charismatic figure, chosen by a group of pirates as their leader over another captain with long experience of raiding in the Indian Ocean.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Describes the qualities and leadership choice of a central figure, Henry Every.\nClues:\n• \"But\" suggests a transition\n• Refers to \"he\", indicating that the person must have been mentioned previously.\n• Narrow description focusing on a specific event related to the central figure.\nSentence 2: \"He was last seen setting out alone, after landing with his remaining shipmates on the north-east coast of Ireland.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Describes the last known actions of the central figure, Henry Every.\nClues:\n• Continues to describe \"he\", which must be Henry Every.\n• specific information about an event in Henry Every's life.\nSentence 3: \"Henry Every remains a phantom—his birth, possibly in Devon, remains obscure, and we don't know when or how he died.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Introduces Henry Every and the mystery surrounding his life.\nClues:\n• introduces Henry Every, providing context for subsequent sentences.\n• Broad introduction, setting the stage for detailed events.\nSentence 4: \"Since he never boarded the Gunsway himself, he is presented as an almost gentlemanly figure — above the fray, but powerless to stop the violence he had unleashed.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Describes Henry Every's reputation and actions related to a specific event.\nClues:\n• Assumes prior mention of his actions or reputation.\n• Narrow, specific event related to his character.\nLogical Sequencing:\n• Sentence 3 should come first as it introduces Henry Every and the mystery surrounding his life, providing a broad overview.\n• Sentence 2 follows as it continues the narrative about Henry Every, specifically his last known actions.\n• Sentence 1 provides details about his leadership and character, making sense after establishing who he is and his final actions.\n• Sentence 4 concludes with a reflection on his actions and reputation, tying together his character description.\nCoherent Paragraph:\nHenry Every remains a phantom—his birth, possibly in Devon, remains obscure, and we don't know when or how he died. He was last seen setting out alone, after landing with his remaining shipmates on the north-east coast of Ireland. But he must have been a charismatic figure, chosen by a group of pirates as their leader over another captain with long experience of raiding in the Indian Ocean. Since he never boarded the Gunsway himself, he is presented as an almost gentlemanly figure — above the fray, but powerless to stop the violence he had unleashed."}, {"n": 22, "type": "TITA", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The four sentences (labelled 1, 2, 3 and 4) given below, when properly sequenced, would yield a coherent paragraph. Decide on the proper sequencing of the order of the sentences and key in the sequence of the four numbers as your answer.\n1. It's business as usual for the military; total global military expenditure reached US$2443 billion in 2023, an increase of 6.8% in real terms from 2022, says the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute (SIPRI) — the steepest year-on-year increase since 2009.\n2. We, like most of Asia south of the Himalaya, are now living on the heating planet which experts have long warned of and science has projected for decades.\n3. Meanwhile, as if nothing untoward was happening, wars in Ukraine and Gaza are — besides death, destruction and misery — producing staggering amounts of greenhouse gases.\n4. On a slow, sweltering summer afternoon at the edge of a forest in deep rural northern Thailand, the brain fever bird is doing my head in with its insistent, rising call.", "options": [], "correct": "4231", "cl_accuracy": 50, "solution": "Correct Answer – 4231\nAnalysis of Each Sentence:\nSentence 1: \"It's business as usual for the military; total global military expenditure reached US$2443 billion in 2023, an increase of 6.8% in real terms from 2022, says the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute (SIPRI) — the steepest year-on-year increase since 2009.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Provides statistical information about global military expenditure.\nClues:\n• \"business as usual\" — Indicates a contrast with the ongoing crises, suggesting military spending continues unchanged.\nSentence 2: \"We, like most of Asia south of the Himalaya, are now living on the heating planet which experts have long warned of and science has projected for decades.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Discusses the broader issue of climate change affecting Asia.\nClues:\n• \"We, like most of Asia\" — Suggests a shared regional experience of climate change.\nSentence 3: \"Meanwhile, as if nothing untoward was happening, wars in Ukraine and Gaza are — besides death, destruction and misery — producing staggering amounts of greenhouse gases.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Describes the ongoing wars and their impact on greenhouse gas emissions.\nClues:\n• \"Meanwhile, as if nothing untoward was happening\" — Implies a simultaneous occurrence of wars, contrasting with the expectation of addressing climate issues.\n• Connects wars with environmental impact, specific to greenhouse gases.\nSentence 4: \"On a slow, sweltering summer afternoon at the edge of a forest in deep rural northern Thailand, the brain fever bird is doing my head in with its insistent, rising call.\"\nWhat the sentence is doing: Sets a scene in rural Thailand, emphasizing the heat.\nClues:\n• Signal words: Descriptive setting —provides context through detailed description.\nLogical Sequencing:\n• Sentence 4 should come first as it sets a vivid scene and introduces the context of heat.\n• Sentence 2 follows as it connects the personal experience of heat in Thailand to the broader issue of climate change.\n• Sentence 3 continues by highlighting how ongoing conflicts exacerbate environmental issues, connecting to the climate change theme.\n• Sentence 1 concludes with a stark contrast, discussing the increase in military expenditure amidst these pressing global issues.\nCoherent Paragraph:\nOn a slow, sweltering summer afternoon at the edge of a forest in deep rural northern Thailand, the brain fever bird is doing my head in with its insistent, rising call. We, like most of Asia south of the Himalaya, are now living on the heating planet which experts have long warned of and science has projected for decades. Meanwhile, as if nothing untoward was happening, wars in Ukraine and Gaza are — besides death, destruction and misery — producing staggering amounts of greenhouse gases. It's business as usual for the military; total global military expenditure reached US$2443 billion in 2023, an increase of 6.8% in real terms from 2022, says the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute (SIPRI) — the steepest year-on-year increase since 2009."}, {"n": 23, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The passage given below is followed by four alternate summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage.\nThe concept of physical time is fundamental to our understanding of the physical world, likely because time itself is an inherent aspect of the world. Philosophers interested in knowledge are inclined to examine the concept of physical time not from a linguistic perspective but rather from an ontological and/or epistemological standpoint, viewing it as a category that encapsulates a basic trait of the world and/or a key component of human knowledge. Ontologically, time can be considered either absolute, meaning it is self-existent, or relative, meaning it is the pace of change. Epistemologically, time can be perceived as subjective, relating to the cognitive subject, or objective, being a feature of the world. Consequently, four consistent theories of time are possible: time as absolute and subjective (as posited by Kant), time as absolute and objective (as proposed by Newton), time as relative and subjective (according to Berkeley), and time as relative and objective (as suggested by Lucretius).", "options": ["Four philosophical theories on time combine absolute/relative and subjective/objective perspectives, proposed by different philosophers.", "Philosophers classify time ontologically and epistemologically, outlining four theories by Kant, Newton, Berkeley, and Lucretius.", "Philosophical study of time presents four consistent theories based on its absolute/relative and subjective/objective nature.", "Inquiry into time's nature leads to four coherent theories, each supported by philosophers like Kant, Newton, Berkeley, and Lucretius."], "correct": "C", "cl_accuracy": 36, "solution": "Correct Answer – (3)\nThe Passage:\nThe passage explains the philosophical exploration of the concept of physical time from ontological (nature of being) and epistemological (nature of knowledge) perspectives. It then details four possible theories of time based on whether it is seen as absolute or relative, and subjective or objective, each associated with philosophers Kant, Newton, Berkeley, and Lucretius.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 3: \"Philosophical study of time presents four consistent theories based on its absolute/relative and subjective/objective nature.\"\n• Comprehensive Summary: This option effectively encapsulates the entirety of the passage. It begins by acknowledging the philosophical study of time, which is the main theme of the passage. The phrase \"presents four consistent theories\" concisely conveys the result of the philosophical exploration described in the passage.\n• Dual Nature of Time: The inclusion of \"based on its absolute/relative and subjective/objective nature\" accurately reflects the dual perspectives (ontological and epistemological) that the passage discusses in depth. These perspectives are crucial to understanding the different theories of time.\n• Implicit Mention of Philosophers: While it doesn't explicitly name the philosophers (Kant, Newton, Berkeley, and Lucretius), it implies their contribution through the discussion of the four consistent theories, which aligns with the passage's content. This keeps the summary concise without omitting essential information.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: \"Four philosophical theories on time combine absolute/relative and subjective/objective perspectives, proposed by different philosophers.\"\nIssue: This option mentions the four theories and their dual perspectives but fails to emphasize the broader philosophical investigation into the concept of time. It also doesn't make it clear that the focus is on the ontological and epistemological aspects, which are key elements of the passage. Furthermore, it states that the perspectives are \"combined,\" which might imply a blend rather than the distinct categorization described in the passage.\nOption 2: \"Philosophers classify time ontologically and epistemologically, outlining four theories by Kant, Newton, Berkeley, and Lucretius.\"\nIssue: This option focuses on the classification of time and mentions the philosophers, but it misses the point that these classifications lead to four consistent theories. It also fails to highlight the nature of these theories (absolute/relative and subjective/objective). While it covers the classification aspect, it does not effectively summarize the resulting theories, making it less comprehensive than Option 3.\nOption 4: \"Inquiry into time's nature leads to four coherent theories, each supported by philosophers like Kant, Newton, Berkeley, and Lucretius.\"\nIssue: This option correctly identifies the four coherent theories and mentions the philosophers but does not specify the dual nature of time (absolute/relative and subjective/objective). It speaks of an \"inquiry into time's nature\" but lacks the clarity and precision regarding the ontological and epistemological perspectives that underpin the passage. Additionally, saying \"supported by\" might be misleading, as it suggests advocacy rather than the development of these theories by the philosophers."}, {"n": 24, "type": "MCQ", "passage_id": null, "stem": "The passage given below is followed by four alternate summaries. Choose the option that best captures the essence of the passage.\nStudies have found that diverse teams feel less comfortable than homogeneous ones, but that they also perform better. Getting there takes real work building an inclusive environment in which each team member can bring their best work. In today's rapidly changing work environment, there are many opportunities to flexibly deploy talent across divisions, functions, teams, and geographies. Whether it is something an individual asks for or the organization's idea, selectively and purposefully taking leaders out of their comfort zone and placing them in situations in which they are in the minority can offer valuable benefits.", "options": ["Diverse teams may feel less comfortable compared to homogeneous ones, highlighting the need for inclusive environments and strategic deployment of talent across different settings to maximise potential.", "While diverse teams might face discomfort, they ultimately deliver better results, necessitating efforts to create inclusive spaces and purposefully placing leaders of minority status to gain valuable benefits.", "The discomfort of diverse teams is outweighed by their superior performance, suggesting the importance of inclusive environments and the positioning of leaders in minority situations to drive optimal results.", "Studies show that diverse teams, though they feel less comfortable, perform better, requiring efforts to build inclusivity and strategically place leaders where they are in the minority to harness their potential."], "correct": "D", "cl_accuracy": 61, "solution": "Correct Answer – (4)\nThe Passage:\nThe passage discusses how diverse teams feel less comfortable than homogeneous ones but perform better. It emphasizes the need for inclusive environments and strategic deployment of leaders into minority situations to achieve better results and maximize potential.\nCorrect Answer Explanation:\nOption 4: \"Studies show that diverse teams, though they feel less comfortable, perform better, requiring efforts to build inclusivity and strategically place leaders where they are in the minority to harness their potential.\"\n• Comprehensive and Accurate: This option captures the core points of the passage: the discomfort and superior performance of diverse teams, the need for inclusive environments, and the strategic placement of leaders in minority situations. It mirrors the passage's key points and maintains clarity and conciseness.\n• Balanced Emphasis: The summary balances the discussion of both the challenges (discomfort) and the strategies for achieving better performance (inclusivity and strategic placement), reflecting the passage's focus accurately.\nIncorrect Answer Explanations:\nOption 1: \"Diverse teams may feel less comfortable compared to homogeneous ones, highlighting the need for inclusive environments and strategic deployment of talent across different settings to maximise potential.\"\n• Issue: This option misses the specific point about placing leaders in minority situations, which is a key strategy discussed in the passage. It also doesn't clearly state that diverse teams perform better, an important aspect of the passage.\nOption 2: \"While diverse teams might face discomfort, they ultimately deliver better results, necessitating efforts to create inclusive spaces and purposefully placing leaders of minority status to gain valuable benefits.\"\n• Issue: This option misinterprets the passage by suggesting \"placing leaders of minority status.\" The passage talks about placing leaders in situations where they are in the minority, not necessarily leaders who are minorities. This is a crucial distinction that changes the meaning.\nOption 3: \"The discomfort of diverse teams is outweighed by their superior performance, suggesting the importance of inclusive environments and the positioning of leaders in minority situations to drive optimal results.\"\n• Issue: This option suggests a trade-off (\"outweighed by\") that the passage does not explicitly state. The passage describes the challenges and benefits without implying a simple trade-off. Additionally, \"drive optimal results\" is less precise than \"harness their potential.\"\n\n---"}]}};
